## 1. The standard and its terms

- **Which:** 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards, Computer Science Teachers Association, published summer
  2026, DOI 10.1145/3820482. It replaces the 2017 CSTA standards. Checked at source 2026-11-16: the PDF behind
  csteachers.org/2026-csta-pk-12-computer-science-standards/ (218 pages).
- **Shape:** three parts. Concepts are the content and the organizing frame: Algorithms and Design (ALG),
  Programming (PRO), Data and Analysis (DAT), Systems and Security (SYS), Computing and Society (SOC). Artificial
  intelligence has no concept of its own; it is spread across all five. Twelve practices in four groups (Ethics and
  Social Responsibility, Inclusive Collaboration, Computational Thinking, Human-Centered Design) say what students
  do. Seven dispositions (creativity, critical thinking, curiosity, persistence, reflectiveness, resourcefulness,
  sense of belonging) are habits of mind. Each standard carries a boundary statement saying what is and is not
  expected; the boundary statement, not the one-line statement, sets the depth (C1, "at the depth its wording asks").
- **Counts:** 196 Foundational ids (EK 16, E1 16, E2 16, E3 19, E4 19, E5 19, MS 45, HS 46; by concept ALG 40,
  PRO 45, DAT 35, SYS 38, SOC 38). 135 Specialty ids in two tiers (S1 73, S2 62) over six areas (AI, cybersecurity,
  data science, game development, physical computing, software development) plus five X + CS standards (computer
  science inside another subject).
- **Terms:** CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, printed on the PDF's license page and every page footer: free to share and adapt with
  credit, non-commercial, adaptations under the same license. Curio Class is free and sells nothing, which is the non-commercial test as we read it (not legal advice); if a paid tier
  ever appears, this is rechecked. Its own pages are CC BY. So: the statement text lives only in data/standards/csta-2026-statements.txt under CSTA's
  license; every Curio Class page, plan and tool cites ids and says things in our own words, and never copies a
  statement or boundary statement. A page states what a standard asks in a new sentence built from the plan's own task,
  never reworded line by line from the statement. Credit line where a day leans on a statement: "Standards: 2026 CSTA PK-12
  Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)". Never say a course is aligned to, reviewed by or endorsed by CSTA.
- **Quoting.** A plan cites each standard by its id and states its action as one verb or a short phrase in our own words
  (the computer science form of UNIT-PLAN rule 15); the statement and boundary statement are never quoted. The
  statement lives in data/standards/csta-2026-statements.txt for the planner, not on the page.
- **Depth.** Each standard's boundary statement (in the PDF) says what students do and what is not expected, and so
  sets the depth. The boundary statements are not stored in the repository. The PDF was read once on 2026-11-21 and
  every owner row in data/progressions/computer-science.csv was checked against its boundary statement then, so each
  owner row's notes carry the depth in our own words, including what is left out. Every plan writes the depth it
  teaches to, and what it leaves out, in our own words, from its owner row. A K to 5 course never teaches an id at its
  next band's depth: for example, grade 4 writes an algorithm in words, and the flowchart is grade 5's.
- **Sub-concept codes** are the third part of an id. A code names a strand inside one concept or area: DAT-IM and
  ALG-IM are different strands. The key below (read from the PDF's naming tables, 2026-11-21) gives each code, its name
  and our one-line meaning.
- **The word AI.** Student copy may name artificial intelligence and machine learning as subjects of study. It never says
  how Curio Class was made.

**Sub-concept key, Foundational.**

| Concept | Code | Name | Our meaning |
|---|---|---|---|
| ALG | PS | Algorithmic problem solving | Planning, writing and checking step-by-step solutions |
| ALG | ML | Machine learning | Systems that find patterns in data to classify, predict or make things |
| ALG | IM | Impacts of algorithms and design | Who an algorithm or a design helps, leaves out or harms |
| PRO | PD | Program development | Building, improving and sharing programs, alone and in teams |
| PRO | VD | Variables and data storage | How a program holds, changes and organizes values |
| PRO | RD | Reading and documenting | Reading, explaining and commenting code |
| PRO | TR | Testing and refining | Testing, debugging and improving a program |
| DAT | DC | Data collection and preparation | Gathering, organizing, checking and cleaning data |
| DAT | DI | Data investigation | Asking a question of data, finding patterns, showing results |
| DAT | IM | Impacts of data science | How collecting and using data affects people |
| SYS | HW | Hardware and software | The parts of a computing system and how they work together |
| SYS | SE | Security | Keeping devices, accounts and information safe |
| SYS | NT | Networks | How devices connect and send information |
| SYS | IM | Impacts of computing systems | How systems affect lives, access and the environment |
| SOC | HI | History of computing | How computing came to be and what it changed |
| SOC | ET | Emerging technologies | New technologies, their limits and their effects |
| SOC | HU | Humans and computing | What people and machines each do, and the choices people make |
| SOC | CE | Career exploration | Computing in work and in students' own plans |

**Sub-concept key, Specialty** (high school; tiers S1 and S2). AIN (artificial intelligence): DD design and
development, DS data science for AI, HR human responsibility, PP professional practice. CYB (cybersecurity): ND network
theory and design, NO network operations, TS threats and security measures, CP cybersecurity policies, PP professional
practice. DSC (data science): CC creation and curation, AM analysis and modeling techniques, MI model interpretation and
reasoning, VZ visualization, PP professional practice. GMD (game development): DD design and development, PX player
experience, AR architecture, PP professional practice. PHY (physical computing): HC hardware and circuit design, IO
inputs and outputs, DD design and development, CI connectivity and the internet of things, PP professional practice.
SWD (software development): DD design and development, UX user experience, TR testing and refining, PP professional
practice. XCS (X + CS): XC, computer science inside another subject. In every area PP means the habits and ethics of
working in the field.

## 2. How the standards map to our courses

Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): one course per grade, K to 12, 13 courses under the
`cs-k12` track. The catalogue keeps that one `cs-k12` row, noted "2026 CSTA PK-12 standards; one course per grade
K-12" (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-cs-1).

| Our course | CSTA band | Ids | Note |
|---|---|---|---|
| Computer Science, kindergarten | EK (pre-K and K) | 16 | Curio Class has no pre-K, so all of EK is taught in K |
| Computer Science, grades 1 to 5 (one course each) | E1 to E5 | 16, 16, 19, 19, 19 | CSTA writes one set per grade; each course teaches its own |
| Computer Science, grades 6, 7 and 8 (one course each) | MS | 45 across the three | CSTA writes one band and no grade split; the progression gives each id one owner grade (see "Ownership of MS and HS ids") |
| Computer Science, grades 9 to 12 (one course each) | HS Foundational | 46 across the four | One owner grade each, by the same rule; Specialty ids claimed only where a unit already goes that far (Cory, 2026-11-11) |

Sessions a week: one in K to 5 (a specials rotation), two to three in 6 to 12 (a real elective). Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-followups-1). Defaults (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-cs-1): K to 2, 32 sessions of 30 minutes; 3 to 5, 32 of 40; 6 to 12, 64 of 45 (two a week) for each year. A course frame may change them with its reason, as PE's defaults allow, and each band's scope and sequence names its short path (bar C2). Why two a week in 6 to 12: each high school year owns only 10 to 12 ids, and three a week would push the days outside the standards past the bar's 10 percent limit.

**Ownership of MS and HS ids.** The course progression (layer 4, docs/PRODUCTION-PATH.md), not the scope and sequence,
places each MS and HS id in exactly one grade that owns it: the grade where students are first taught it to the depth
set under "Depth" in section 1, and checked on it. Another grade of the band that uses the id again lists it as
`~ID` (used in a new setting, contributes). Every MS id is owned by grade 8 at the latest, every HS id by grade 12.
Starting split (ours, untested; counted from data/standards/csta-2026-ids.txt, MS 45 and HS 46): grade 6 owns about 17
MS ids, grade 7 about 15, grade 8 about 13, by concept ALG 4/4/3, DAT 4/2/3, PRO 3/3/3, SOC 3/3/2, SYS 3/3/2 (MS
concepts ALG 11, DAT 9, PRO 9, SOC 8, SYS 8; the split is forced, because MS-DAT-DC-21 to 24 must all come before MS-ALG-ML-07 in grade 7, which puts four DAT ids in grade 6). Grades 9, 10 and 11 own 12 HS ids each and grade 12 owns 10 (HS concepts ALG 11, DAT 8, PRO 9, SOC 9,
SYS 9; HS-DAT-IM-27 moved to grade 11, Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1), with every concept in every year. Ordering constraints, ours: MS-PRO-VD-16 before MS-ALG-PS-01 and MS-PRO-RD-17;
MS-PRO-PD-12 before MS-PRO-RD-17; MS-PRO-RD-17 before MS-PRO-RD-18; MS-DAT-DC-23 before MS-DAT-DI-25 to 27; MS-ALG-ML-06
before MS-ALG-ML-07 and 08; MS-DAT-DC-21 to 24 before MS-ALG-ML-07; MS-SYS-NT-34 before NT-35; MS-DAT-DI-25 owned in
grade 8 unless the math frame teaches association earlier (from memory). HS: HS-PRO-VD-16 before HS-ALG-PS-01;
HS-PRO-PD-12 before HS-PRO-RD-17; HS-ALG-ML-07 before ML-08; HS-ALG-PS-03 after PS-01 and 02; HS-SYS-SE-31 and 32
before SE-33; HS-SOC-ET-40 and 41 before ET-42; HS-DAT-IM-27 before IM-28. Stems line up only inside K to 5 (a stem such
as E3-PRO-PD-04 follows E2-PRO-PD-04); MS and HS numbers do not match E5's, so a writer never assumes the same stem
across E5 to MS. EK is taught whole in kindergarten and E1 repeats several of its constructs: the K to 1 handoff is marked
weak in the progression, not re-taught.

**Partial base for K to 8.** Settled 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): the UK's open computing
curriculum (scout: docs/reviews/computer-science-open-material-2026-11-16.md) is a partial base for grades K to 8.
Each unit taken from it is checked unit by unit for third-party material (anything not under the base's own license
is left out), and is rephrased for an American audience when needed (spelling, money, place names, school years).
Its source goes in the day's credit; no company name appears in student, teacher or family copy. The edition the
frames map is the archived Teach Computing Curriculum (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived, resources dated 2023, no
longer maintained by its publisher); England's current lessons, on Oak National Academy, differ by unit, so a frame
that adapts a current unit names it as such (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md).

**Specialty standards.** The four high school courses teach all 46 Foundational ids between them. A unit may also claim an S1 id when
its task already does what the S1 statement and boundary ask (for example a unit that trains and tests a classifier
may claim an AI S1 id). No unit is lengthened to reach a Specialty id, and no S2 id is claimed in a Foundational
course. Decided (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): grades 11 and 12 claim a short named list of
S1 ids as in-course standards, taught and checked in full and counted so bar criterion C1's 10 percent is not reached;
no new courses; S2 stays out. The list is eight S1 ids, four in grade 11 (S1-AIN-DD-02, S1-DSC-VZ-11, S1-CYB-TS-08, S1-SWD-TR-07) and four in grade 12 (S1-AIN-HR-09, S1-DSC-PP-13, S1-SWD-PP-08, S1-SWD-UX-05), each taught inside a row that already teaches the topic. S1 days count as days outside the course's own standards under C1, so each grade's scope and sequence keeps them, with any other such days, under 10 percent of its teaching days. Specialty courses (a pathway of one or two courses per area, as CSTA suggests) are not on the course list;
adding any is Cory's call.

**The 2017 codes** are the superseded set. Tool specs may show them beside 2026 ids as a crosswalk; they never stand
alone.

## 3. What is left out, and why

- Pre-K: no Curio Class course. The EK set is taught whole in kindergarten.
- What a school may swap or leave out (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-cs-1; the same rule as PE and the arts): a school
  may swap or leave out a named unit for its community's reasons. Each course page names these units, and it claims no
  standard that no remaining unit teaches; leaving out a unit means the course page and the unit's family note name
  the ids not claimed that year.
- Specialty II in the Foundational course (see above), and Specialty courses as courses (not on the list).
- The X + CS standards (XCS) as computer science days: they describe computer science inside other subjects, so
  they are leans for those subjects' units, not this course's.
- Boundary statements as text: never copied (license and our own voice). Plans restate the depth in our own words.
- Practices and dispositions have no ids in data/standards; units name the practice by its code and our own words,
  and every unit names at least one practice it teaches, with its day and task (bar criterion 2). The PDF pairs each
  standard with one or two practices and one or two dispositions; that pairing is not stored. The twelve practices,
  numbered 1 to 12 across four groups (read from the PDF 2026-11-21; labels ours), and which of the bar's four items
  (working together, testing and fixing, abstraction, explaining) each serves:

| Code | Group | Our label | Bar item |
|---|---|---|---|
| ESR1 | Ethics and social responsibility | Use computing for good: weigh who gains and who is harmed | none |
| ESR2 | Ethics and social responsibility | Respect people: credit others' work, protect users' data | none |
| IC3 | Inclusive collaboration | Explain computing ideas, give and take feedback | explaining |
| IC4 | Inclusive collaboration | Run a project: shared goals, tasks, milestones, documented work | working together |
| IC5 | Inclusive collaboration | Be a reliable, respectful teammate and reflect on the team's work | working together |
| CT6 | Computational thinking | Define the problem: criteria, limits, smaller parts | abstraction |
| CT7 | Computational thinking | Abstract: find patterns, make reusable parts, model and simulate | abstraction |
| CT8 | Computational thinking | Build: compare approaches, plan, then code or train | none |
| CT9 | Computational thinking | Test, debug and refine | testing and fixing |
| HCD10 | Human-centered design | Learn from and include different users | none |
| HCD11 | Human-centered design | Prototype, test and refine for use and access | testing and fixing |
| HCD12 | Human-centered design | Keep users informed and in control | none |

## 4. Cross-subject leans on the core progressions

CSTA prints interdisciplinary links for each Foundational standard (to CCSS, NGSS, the arts, health, library and
career standards). Our own reading of where the course leans is below; the skill ids of each lean on a core
progression are in the progression's `cross_subject` column (data/progressions/computer-science.csv), where the
check tests them. Library and career education (the last two lines) are not core subjects, so they are named here
only, not as progression leans.

- **Math:** sequence and pattern (K to 2), coordinate grids and angles for movement tasks (grades 4 to 6), variables
  and expressions (grade 6 on), data displays and statistics (DAT throughout), Boolean conditions as true and false
  statements. Frame "if later" lines bridge where the math grade has not taught it yet.
- **Science:** data collection and analysis, models and simulations, and the engineering design standards (ETS),
  which share the define, test and improve loop with ALG design standards.
- **ELA:** explaining an algorithm in writing, giving and taking feedback, research for SOC topics.
- **Social studies:** SOC standards on the history and effects of computing, privacy, law and policy (high school
  government); the values rule applies to them.
- **Library and research skills:** judging online sources, citing and using others' work (AASL Curate and Engage);
  the library course owns the method, computer science uses it.
- **Career education:** the Digital Technology cluster (CTE.Career Clusters.Digital Technology and its sub-clusters).

## 5. Cost, devices and rooms

Programming units run on a device per one or two students in a common browser; nothing is installed and no account is
made (bar criterion 7). Physical computing (a microcontroller board) is optional and always has a simulator or paper
stand-in; its cost is stated where used.

### Devices and rooms

Three things are kept apart. An unplugged day teaches an idea with cards, paper or movement; every unit has at least
one (kind: understanding; bar item 5). A device day is a day where a tool does a named job. A stand-in is how a device
day runs with fewer devices (C14). Every device day is written four ways (section fields 5 and 10): (1) one device per
pair, the default; (2) a few devices: three or four stations on a rotation, the other pairs at a paper station on the
same idea, every pair taking the keyboard role at least once; (3) one screen: the class runs the program together,
predicts and votes before each run, and a student at the device takes each step; (4) no device: the same idea on paper,
with trace tables, "be the computer", programs written on paper and checked by a partner. An id whose standard asks
students to use a computational tool, train a model, or use documentation and libraries is met only on a device (for
example E4-DAT-DC-09, E5-DAT-DC-09, E5-ALG-ML-02, MS-DAT-DC-23, MS-DAT-DI-25, MS-PRO-PD-13, HS-DAT-DC-21,
HS-DAT-DC-23, HS-ALG-ML-08, HS-PRO-PD-12, HS-PRO-PD-13, HS-SYS-HW-30). On the no-device path that day teaches the
idea and the course record marks the id "part met without a device"; the course never says the id was met.

### Generated-text tools

Evaluating generated output (MS-PRO-RD-18, HS-PRO-RD-18, HS-ALG-PS-05) is met on supplied material: Curio Class supplies
saved outputs and code samples, some right and some flawed, and students judge them for accuracy and use. No tool
asks a student to use a live generated-text tool, make an account or send anything off the device. For MS-ALG-PS-05,
which asks students to use one (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): no live tool in Curio
Class; the day is teacher-run on one screen with a tool the school has already approved and no student accounts, and
the course marks the id "part met" where a school has none.

### Our own editor

Settled (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-prework-open-1): build one small browser editor of our own (blocks and a
text view, with a step-through trace), a single file with no account, starting with grades 3 to 5, rather than pointing
teachers to a third-party tool. The text language for grades 6 to 12 is Python, run in this editor (Cory, 2026-11-21,
Desk q-fix-cs-1): the K to 8 partial base teaches Python in its middle years, so its units adapt directly, and it is
the most common first text language in high school.

The editor also keeps a small offline version history with branch, review and merge, so grade 12's team project
meets S1-SWD-PP-08 in full; until that exists, the standard is part met and the course page says so (Cory, 2026-11-21,
Desk q-fix-cs-2). It is an ask for the editor's own build list when the editor is specced.

### Blocks to text

Kindergarten to grade 5: block code with a text view of the same program. Grade 6: blocks and Python text side by
side. Grade 7: written Python for new programs, blocks as a stand-in. Grade 8 on: Python. Python is named as a subject
term on teacher pages and runs in our own editor in a common browser with nothing installed. The language is Cory's
(above); the grade steps are ours, untested.

### Privacy and safety

No student's name, photo, voice, location or family detail goes into a program, a dataset or a tool. Class data is about
things (weather, pets drawn from a list, shapes, snack types), tallied by hand or with counts, never about identifiable
children, health or home. Accounts, passwords and personal information are taught with a pretend page or a paper account;
no student makes an account. Online safety days use paper or an offline pretend site; any real web use is teacher-led on
one screen. Security days use toy systems built into the page; no scanning, no attack and no test of a real school or
home system, ever. A tool stores student work only on the device or in a file the student downloads or prints; it sends
nothing off the device.

### Real cases in society days

A real case is told by what happened, not by the company or product: "a company sold a face-matching system to police;
a 2018 study found it wrong more often for darker-skinned women" (example only, from memory, facts to be checked
before use). The teacher page gives the date and the source's title and publisher in a source line, which is a citation
and not a promotion. If a case cannot be understood without the name, choose another case or use a court or government
record's own description. A society day gives two views and one dated source for each.
Decided (Cory, 2026-11-20, Desk q-prework-cs-1): student and teacher copy may name government bodies and
courts but never companies, with sources only in the teacher source line.
# Computer science: approach (layer 3)

**Approved by Cory 2026-11-20 (Desk q-prework-cs-1). Written 2026-11-16, fixed 2026-11-19 after a cold read
(docs/reviews/prework-computer-science-read-2026-11-19.md); fields placed by number and sensitive ground gathered
2026-11-21 (docs/reviews/prework-computer-science-fixes-2026-11-20.md).** It fills the subject slots of the unit and
section plan (docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md) and leans on the shared approach (docs/APPROACH.md), the lens
(docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md) and the standard (STANDARD.md), which holds the rules this page points to:
ownership of MS and HS ids, depth, devices and rooms, privacy and safety, blocks to text, real cases, quoting. Ours,
untested.

## The kinds of day, in computer science's words

The `kind` column keeps the shared words; the goal says the activity.

| Shared kind | In computer science | What it looks like |
|---|---|---|
| Understanding | Unplugged or trace day | An idea taught with cards, paper or movement, or a given program traced and predicted before it is run |
| Fluency | Modify day | Students change a working program or algorithm in small, checked steps; short practice with the construct |
| Application | Make day, design day or society day | Students design and build their own program or artifact against criteria, or audit a real system or case with evidence |
| Check | Assessment day | The end-of-unit assessment (unit field 5, below) |
| Respond | Respond day | Fixes from the assessment, by error letter |

Order inside a unit: understanding before modify before make. A make day never comes before a trace or modify day on
the same construct.

## How a day opens (section field 6)

Every day opens in its first five minutes with one of three things. (a) A retrieval trace: a short program or algorithm
from an earlier unit, "what happens?", answered on paper, three minutes. (b) The wrong example from the last exit
ticket, if more than a third missed it. (c) For a new idea, one short concrete moment, unplugged, that the teaching then
names. Teaching first is the default for anything new: no blank editor, and no "try it" on a construct, before one worked
example has been shown and traced. In grades 6 to 12 a day may open with a predict task on a short given program (ten
minutes at most; ours, untested) when students have all they need to attempt it. The day row gives the opening and its
minutes.

## The form of daily evidence

- **Grades K to 2:** the answer is a pointing, sorting or ordering task, or said aloud to a partner and noted by the
  teacher by exception; a program is checked on the screen during the day.
- **Grades 3 to 12, trace or predict days:** an exit ticket with a short program or algorithm and the question "what
  happens?", answered in writing, with wrong answers by error letter (UNIT-PLAN rule 3).
- **Modify and make days:** the program itself, checked against two or three stated look-fors, plus one line from the
  student: what they tested and what they changed. A tool checks the look-fors it can (does the program do these three
  things on the tests?) and shows the student a "yes" or "not yet" for each; the teacher reads the open part for a
  rotating third of the class each session, so every student is read at least once in every three sessions (in K to
  5, which meets once a week, that is once in three weeks), and at the next session as well if the exit ticket shows
  a gap.
- **Unplugged days:** the paper artifact (the sorted cards, the drawn path, the encoded message) with one written
  question (K to 2: said or pointed).
- **Society days:** a short claim and two views, each with one dated source (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days").
- Student work is read on the device, or on a printout the student hands in.

## What computer science adds to the plan fields

Numbered as in docs/courses/UNIT-PLAN.md. Fields not named here (unit fields 1, 3, 4, 7 and 9; section fields 2 and 11)
are filled as UNIT-PLAN.md says, with nothing added for computer science.

**Unit plan.**
- Field 2: each standard's parts by verb (the id and our own words, STANDARD.md "Quoting"); the limits: the depth
  taught to and what is left out (STANDARD.md "Depth", from the owner row's notes); the practice the unit teaches,
  named by its code and our label (STANDARD.md section 3); what is
  carried through the unit: the anchor program, algorithm or dataset (its name, the day it is shown whole, the day it
  is withdrawn, the day students first build alone); the unit's unplugged days, counted.
- Field 5: read and trace items plus a short build or debug task (a society unit: a short audit or claim task). The
  task has look-fors and two sample programs or pieces, one that meets them and one that does not, and a paper version
  for a class with no device, marked as in STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms".
- Field 6: the unit's common bugs and notional-machine errors are lettered here with the other errors, one mistake
  per letter, each with what it shows.
- Field 8: each tool with its day, mark, job and verdict, and a link to the course's TOOL-ASKS.md; devices needed per
  pair; the supplies list, with the optional board's cost where one is used (STANDARD.md section 5); one line for the
  school's technical staff (a common browser, one file, nothing installed, no account, nothing sent off the device).
  **What a tool may and may not do:** a tool may step through a program line by line, run stated tests on a student
  program and show "yes" or "not yet" for each look-for, show blocks and text views of one program, and run a toy
  network, model or security system built into the page. Everything a tool may not do is set in STANDARD.md section
  5 ("Privacy and safety", "Generated-text tools") and under "The form of daily evidence" (the teacher reads the open
  part).
- Field 10: for a unit taken from the partial base, the base unit and its third-party check (STANDARD.md section 2,
  "Partial base for K to 8"); for each society case, its dated source (STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days").

**Section plan.**
- Field 1: the notional-machine point (what the computer does with each line, in order) or, in a unit with no code, the
  adult-level background; the practice, its day and its task; in a section on sensitive ground, its teacher note (see
  "Sensitive ground").
- Field 3: each day's evidence in the form set under "The form of daily evidence".
- Field 4: each expected error as a wrong example beside a right one: a wrong program for code, a wrong statement for a
  systems, data or society idea.
- Field 5: what is carried and what happens to it: the whole worked program, then the same program with blanks or
  shuffled lines, then modified by students, then withdrawn; the key moments and the tool that serves each; what a room
  with nothing does: the few-device and no-device versions of each device day (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms").
- Field 6: one row a day: kind in this page's words; how the day opens and for how long; who holds the worked program
  (teacher, pair, student); tool part, mark, job and minutes at the device; work away from the device; the evidence
  number.
- Field 7: two or three planned questions, at least one of the form "what will happen if"; one written or spoken task
  ("what I tested and what I changed"); its look-fors. Pair roles (driver, navigator) and the time they switch go here.
- Field 8: practice that returns: a three-minute trace or debug of an earlier unit's construct, the unit named. No
  fluency target.
- Field 9: English learners, access, check accommodations, reading load and extension, as set under "Supports".
- Field 10: two rooms: one child at home, where the parent takes the other pair role (driver or navigator) and they
  switch when field 7 says; one screen in the room, the one-screen version of each device day (STANDARD.md "Devices
  and rooms").

**Teacher page for a unit with code** carries: the idea at adult level; a finished, tested solution to every program
students write; each common bug as a wrong program beside a right one with what it shows; the exact message the tool
shows for each bug; and "if you are stuck" steps a teacher can follow without writing code. A teacher is never asked to
debug a student's program on the fly.

## Supports (section field 9)

- **English learners.** Each unit names five to eight words taught in depth (for example step, repeat, if, variable,
  bug), each with a picture or a traced example, and two sentence frames: "First the program ___, then it ___" and "I
  predict ___ because line ___ says ___". Words with an everyday meaning (run, loop, bug, sort, cloud, mouse) are taught
  as two meanings. A student may answer a trace in numbers, arrows or the home language. Each key says which wrong
  answers can come from reading the words rather than from the idea, so a language gap is not marked as a content gap.
- **Access.** Every tool works by keyboard alone, needs no color to tell things apart (shape or label as well),
  enlarges text without breaking, has no flashing, and offers a typed or list choice wherever it offers drag and drop;
  every block program has a text view. For a student who cannot see the screen, the unplugged version uses cards and
  objects they can feel, and code is read aloud one line at a time. No tool task is timed.
- **Reading load.** Grades K to 2: no code read alone (icons, read aloud). Grades 3 to 5: up to ten lines. Grades 6 to 12:
  up to twenty lines (ours, untested). Error messages are plain words we write.
- **Check accommodations.** Read aloud, oral explanation, a scribe, extra time at the device, the paper version.
- **Extension.** On the same standard: a second bug, a new case the program must handle, an explanation of why a second
  solution is clearer. Never the next grade's construct.

## Sensitive ground

These rules gather what the computer science pages and progression rows already say. A section that touches one
carries its teacher note in section field 1: what may come up in the room, and how to keep the talk on how a system
was built, tested and used, never on a group or a classmate. Units with cultural, Native or disability content are
read by the sensitivity agent before they go live (docs/FOUNDATION.md section 7; docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md).

- **Bias, exclusion and access.** Who a system serves, leaves out or treats worse (for example E4-ALG-IM-03,
  E5-SOC-HI-16, MS-ALG-IM-10 and 11, MS-SYS-IM-37, MS-SOC-ET-41 and 42, HS-ALG-IM-10, HS-SOC-ET-41). Cases follow
  STANDARD.md "Real cases in society days": what happened, two views, a dated source for each, no company named.
- **Data about people and communities.** Personal data, large-scale collection and data sovereignty (MS-DAT-IM-28,
  HS-DAT-IM-27 and 28). Class data follows STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety"; a case about a Native nation's or other
  community's data gets the sensitivity read above.
- **Online safety and personal information.** Accounts, sharing and online actions (E1-SYS-SE-12, E2-SYS-SE-12,
  E3-SYS-SE-13, MS-SOC-HU-43) are taught as STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety" sets: a pretend page or a paper account.
- **Work and automation.** Jobs made or replaced by automation (MS-SOC-CE-45) can touch a student's family; the
  teacher note says so.
- **If a student tells you something.** Computer science uses health's steps, word for word
  (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"); every teacher page of an online safety or
  personal information section carries them, as PE's do (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-pe-1).
- **What a school may swap or leave out:** STANDARD.md section 3 (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-cs-1), the same rule
  as PE and the arts.

## Pacing

Each course frame states weeks, sessions a week and minutes a session; the defaults and the short path are in
STANDARD.md section 2 (Cory, 2026-11-21, Desk q-fix-cs-1). Because K to 5 meet once a week, every session
opens with the three-minute retrieval trace and every unit ends with a respond day. Ours, untested: kindergarten to
grade 2 spend about two thirds of the time away from a device; grades 3 to 5 about half; grades 6 to 12 at least one
unplugged day in every unit. A K to 5 unit lets two or three ids share one anchor program, so one id is not one
session. Which ids a high school year owns is set in STANDARD.md "Ownership of MS and HS ids".

## Honest limits

The order of days (read and predict, then modify, then make) follows PRIMM (predict, run, investigate, modify, make),
read for this page on 2026-11-21. What that evidence supports, and no more: one quasi-experimental study (2018, about
500 students aged 11 to 14 in English schools, 8 to 12 weeks of Python lessons on sequence, selection and iteration,
against classes taught the same topics the school's usual way) found higher post-test scores for the PRIMM classes,
and interviews with teachers found they valued the shared structure and the talk about code. The groups were not
randomised, the evidence is for ages 11 to 14 in text-based Python, and the authors say which parts make the difference
is still unknown. Our use in kindergarten to grade 5 (block and unplugged days) and our session counts are ours and
untested. Two ideas from the same sources are already in this page and are now grounded: reading and tracing code before
writing it, and pair or small-group talk about a given program away from the device. No page claims that learning to
program improves thinking in general or school results. The course has not been tried with children. A one-page logic
model is owed at course level. Sources read for this page are listed in docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md "Computer science
approach reads, 2026-11-21".

## Settled

- 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-cs-career-library): one course per grade K to 12, and the UK computing
  curriculum as a partial base for K to 8 (STANDARD.md section 2).
- 2026-11-16 (Cory, Desk card q-later-followups-1): one session a week in K to 5, two to three a week in 6 to 12.
- 2026-11-20 (Cory, Desk q-prework-cs-1): this page approved as the layer 3 page for computer science, with the cold
  read's fixes in.
- 2026-11-21 (Cory, Desk q-prework-open-1): our own editor (STANDARD.md "Our own editor").
- 2026-11-21 (Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1): default minutes and sessions a year (STANDARD.md section 2); the swap and
  leave-out rule (STANDARD.md section 3); Python as the text language for grades 6 to 12 (STANDARD.md "Our own
  editor"); the one `cs-k12` catalogue row.

## Open for Cory

Nothing at present.
# Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 3

Course `computer-science-grade-3` (catalogue id `cs-k12`), grade 3, one year. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, written on the shape of the kindergarten pilot (docs/courses/computer-science-kindergarten/frame.md and its fix report) and matched to the grade 2 frame's handoffs; the scope and sequence sets the days later.

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 5, one for each concept area at its grade 3 depth, in the order the ideas build: software, the internet
  and what we share (systems and security, with the benefits and risks of widely used systems, so the system-diagram,
  the network-diagram and the device rules sit before the first program), algorithms that choose (algorithms, the
  first choice on step-cards, and how two algorithms for one job treat different people), programs that choose
  (programming, turning unit 2's step-card algorithms into block programs, then improving, reading, tracing and
  debugging them), checking data and asking everyone (data, with machine learning on the class's own training set),
  and computing at work, then and now (computing and society, the year's closer).
- **Days.** 32 course days: 28 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 2 respond
  days (one after each check). One lesson a week of about 40 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for
  grades 3 to 5 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), a specials rotation, so
  course day n falls in week n. The 40 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: a session opens with a
  three-minute retrieval trace from the second session on (APPROACH.md, Pacing), and device sessions keep four
  minutes at the end to save or print work, shut devices and collect papers, so a session teaches for about 33
  minutes. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment and end with a respond day (APPROACH.md, Pacing), with
  the pilot's one shape for a unit that a course-level check follows directly: in units 2 and 4 the check stands for
  the unit's own end-of-unit assessment and the check's respond day is the unit's respond day (pilot For the lead 2).
  So units 1 to 5 teach on 4, 4, 7, 4 and 3 days, 22 of the 32. Days follow the load of the rows: unit 3 owns four
  programming rows and five ids, each with a device version and the worked, modify, make, feedback, read, trace and
  debug order, and gets 9 days; unit 1 owns four rows and five ids, but three of its rows are single unplugged
  sessions and the two ids of g3-benefits-risks share one chart, so it gets 6; units 2 and 4 get 4 teaching sessions
  for two and four rows; unit 5's two rows carry three ids, one session each, and get 5. Grade 3 owns 19 ids in the
  same 32 sessions that grade 2 used for 16; the 40-minute session (10 more than kindergarten to grade 2) carries the
  three extra ids, so no unit is shorter than the grade 2 unit of the same concept area. Each course-level check takes
  one check session and one respond session, since a 40-minute session holds a written sitting of about 25 minutes
  and short spoken answers for the children who need them. No unit runs over 25 days, so there are no mid-unit
  assessments.
- **Check weeks.** The grade 3 core checks take weeks 9, 12, 14, 16, 20, 23, 25, 28, 30, 31 and 32
  (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md, target weeks: ELA 16 and 32, math 12, 23 and 31, science 9, 20 and 30, social studies
  14, 25 and 28). This course's checks sit in week 11 (mid-year, after unit 2) and week 26 (year-end, after unit 4),
  with their respond days in weeks 12 and 27 (respond days are not check days). Week 26 lets unit 5 have the five
  weeks 28 to 32 its three society ids need, as the grade 2 frame does. Unit 3 runs across weeks 13 to 21, which
  include the core check weeks 14, 16 and 20, because it holds no course-level check; its own end-of-unit assessment
  falls in week 20 and unit 5's in week 31, unit assessments, not course-level checks. Overlaps with other after-core
  subjects' check weeks are the lead's calendar pass (For the lead 1).
- **Standard.** The 19 E3 ids of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards, all taught here (STANDARD.md
  section 2): E3-ALG-PS-01, E3-ALG-ML-02, E3-ALG-IM-03, E3-PRO-PD-04, E3-PRO-PD-05, E3-PRO-VD-06, E3-PRO-RD-07,
  E3-PRO-TR-08, E3-DAT-DC-09, E3-DAT-DI-10, E3-DAT-IM-11, E3-SYS-HW-12, E3-SYS-SE-13, E3-SYS-NT-14, E3-SYS-IM-15,
  E3-SOC-HI-16, E3-SOC-ET-17, E3-SOC-HU-18, E3-SOC-CE-19. Each owned id's boundary was read in the source store's
  standards PDF for this frame (2026-11-25, S:\CurioClass-sources\computer-science\csta-2026-11-21, not copied or
  quoted), and each is taught at the depth its owner row's notes set, in our own words (STANDARD.md "Depth"); "What
  students learn" says what is out. Plans cite ids and state each action in a short phrase of our own (STANDARD.md
  "Quoting"); the credit line "Standards: 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)" goes on any
  day that leans on a statement. Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the computer science bar
  (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 5, items 1 to 9) and the lens (docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md), and
  reads the partial base unit it adapts for order; the "Check set adds" column names what the writer expects that to
  add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. No Specialty id, and no X + CS id, is claimed.
- **Practices and concept areas.** The Units table's fifth column (the C3 column of the core frames) names, for
  computer science, the concept area each unit teaches and the CSTA practices it teaches inside the content, by code
  and our label (STANDARD.md section 3), as bar items 1 and 2 ask. Each unit plan names the practice's day and task.
  Across the year: CT6 define the problem (units 1, 2, 3, 4), CT7 find patterns and make reusable parts (units 3, 4),
  CT8 build and plan (units 2, 3), CT9 test, debug and refine (units 3, 4), IC3 explain and give feedback (units 1 to
  5), IC5 be a reliable teammate and take feedback (unit 3, driver and navigator, the feedback trade), HCD10 learn
  from and include different users (units 4, 5), HCD12 keep users informed and in control (units 1, 4, 5), ESR1 weigh
  who gains and who is harmed (units 1, 2, 4, 5), ESR2 respect people, credit others' work and protect information
  (units 1, 3). Dispositions are named in the unit plans, not here.
- **Base text.** Partial: England's open computing curriculum, the Teach Computing Curriculum, archived edition
  (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2, resources dated 2023), the same fixed edition the kindergarten and
  grade 2 frames map. Year 4 is the grade 3 age (the grade 2 frame maps Year 3), and this frame also takes the Year 3
  lessons the grade 2 frame offered on (its For the lead 2: "Connecting computers" lessons 4 to 6, "Branching
  databases" lessons 3 and 4). License as the pilot states: the Open Government Licence v3.0 "unless expressly stated
  otherwise" on the publisher's terms page; the archived unit pages carry no license line of their own, so each
  file's license line is read when the store folder is made. Adapted unit by unit and rephrased for an American
  audience (STANDARD.md section 2, Cory 2026-11-16): US spelling, school years named as US grades, no money in pounds,
  no product or company name, our own browser editor or paper in place of the named block and turtle languages and the
  named data loggers. Three of its six Year 4 units feed units 1, 3 and 4 (see Source map); its two creating-media
  units and its text-turtle unit are left out. Units 2 (but for one Year 3 lesson pair) and 5 are our own. The
  source's name appears only in a day's credit line, once a day.
  Pending(docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md#teach-computing-curriculum): the source store holds no copy of this base, so the
  Source map below rests on the public unit pages (lesson titles, opened 2026-11-25; the lessons' content is read from
  their titles and unit overviews), and each adapted lesson's check for third-party images, text and tool screenshots
  waits on the store folder (For the lead 2).
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
  Understanding is an unplugged or trace session, the first teaching of a construct or idea (a step-card algorithm
  with a yes or no question and two paths, acted out by a partner; a given block program traced and predicted before
  it runs; a trace-table filled by hand). Fluency is a modify session: change one card or one block of a working
  algorithm or program and check it; grade 3 has a little of it (unit 2 session 2, unit 3 session 2, and the
  retrieval openings). Application is a make, design or society session: write a step-card algorithm for a game
  (unit 2 session 3), build it as a block program and improve it from feedback (unit 3 sessions 3 and 4), debug
  given programs with the method (unit 3 session 7), design a fair class survey (unit 4 session 3), or compare two
  algorithms by their effect on people (unit 2 session 4). Check is the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level
  check; Respond acts on it by error letter. Inside a unit: understanding before modify before make, and no make day
  before a trace or modify day on the same construct.
- **Evidence.** Grades 3 to 5 write (APPROACH.md, "The form of daily evidence"): a trace or predict session ends in a
  written exit ticket with a short program or algorithm and "what happens?", wrong answers sorted by error letter; a
  modify or make session's evidence is the program or algorithm itself against two or three look-fors, plus the
  student's line "what I tested and what I changed"; an unplugged session's evidence is the paper artifact with one
  written question; a society session's is a short written claim with a reason. The teacher reads the open part for a
  rotating third of the class each session, so every student is read at least once in three weeks. Every end-of-unit
  assessment is a sitting with unseen items, never the unit's own anchor: a written paper task of about 25 minutes
  (read and trace items, a short debug task, a sort or a short written claim), with a scribe, read-aloud or spoken
  answer for any child who needs it (APPROACH.md, check accommodations). Each owned id has its own look-for: a
  "create", "develop" or "design" id (E3-ALG-PS-01, E3-PRO-PD-04, E3-DAT-IM-11) is met by the child's own algorithm,
  program or plan, never by recognition of a supplied one; "use feedback" (E3-PRO-PD-05) by the child's record of a
  suggestion taken and the change made; "debug" (E3-PRO-TR-08) by finding and fixing a bug; "identify" (E3-PRO-VD-06)
  by naming the variables and filling a trace-table; "articulate", "explain", "describe" and "examine" ids by a short
  written answer with a reason. Unit 3's end-of-unit assessment pairs the sitting with the pair program built and
  improved in sessions 3 and 4 (the draft and the feedback revision are counted in the unit's days), scored on day
  20 against its look-fors and each child's own line. Units 2 and 4 are assessed in the course-level check that
  follows them (Days). No score comes from a session that first teaches a row. The respond day reteaches by error
  letter and repeats the missed item in a new form.
- **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, never needed per child. Device days: unit 1
  session 1 (one task traced from input, through the software, to the screen) and session 2 on one screen (a
  teacher-led search and a pictured video call), unit 3 sessions 1 to 4 and 7 (the block editor with the
  block-and-text-view and a step-through run), with sessions 5 and 6 on a device or on printouts, unit 4 session 2 (a
  graph tool in pairs or on one screen) and session 4's optional one-screen sorter. That leaves about three quarters
  of the year's time away from a device, more than APPROACH.md's "about half" for grades 3 to 5 (ours, untested): the
  E3 rows for data, systems and society are written unplugged first, and the boundaries of E3-DAT-DC-09 and
  E3-DAT-DI-10 do not ask for technology; the programming unit carries the device time. Every device day is written
  four ways (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"): one device per pair, a few devices at stations with the other pairs on
  the paper version of the same idea, one screen with a child at the device for each step, or no device. Which ids
  need a device: the E3-PRO-PD-04 boundary places the student's code in a block-based environment, so on the
  no-device path, where paper block cards are laid and a partner runs them as the computer, the course record marks
  E3-PRO-PD-04 "part met without a device" (an item-level pick, For the lead 4). Every other E3 id is met in full on
  paper: printed programs with their text view, traced with a pencil and a trace-table, meet E3-PRO-VD-06,
  E3-PRO-RD-07 and E3-PRO-TR-08; feedback on a paper program meets E3-PRO-PD-05; the class acting as the sorter meets
  E3-ALG-ML-02, whose boundary asks students to observe, not to train.
- **Paper path.** Every session runs on paper, cards and objects; unit 3's block programs have paper block cards and
  a printed block-and-text-view beside them, and unit 4's sorter is the class itself acting as the machine. No tool
  asks for a student account, stores a child's name or sends anything off the device; the search in unit 1 is
  teacher-led on one screen (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety").
- **Reading level and vocabulary.** Grade 3 children read short texts; code read alone is ten lines at most
  (APPROACH.md, Reading load), and every block carries an icon and a short word. The block-and-text-view shows the
  text beside the blocks to be read, never typed (the E3-PRO-PD-04 boundary keeps text-based languages out). Each unit
  names five to eight words taught in depth, with the everyday words that have a computing meaning taught as two
  meanings (if, else, loop, bug, cloud, variable). The word variable is first used in unit 3 session 6, built on grade
  2's named box; assignment is read as "take what is in score and put one more into it", never "score equals score
  plus 1". Sentence frames: "If ___, then ___, else ___", "I predict ___ because line ___ says ___" and "This part
  makes ___ happen; without it ___". A child may answer a trace in numbers, arrows or the home language.
- **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
  reasons. Unit 5 may be left out: E3-SOC-CE-19, E3-SOC-HI-16 and E3-SOC-HU-18 are then not claimed that year, and the
  course still teaches computing and society through E3-SOC-ET-17 and E3-SYS-IM-15 in unit 1 and E3-ALG-IM-03 in
  unit 2; its cases may instead be swapped for workers and tools of the school's own community (the same three ids,
  one case set a session). Unit 4's survey topic and data tables may be swapped for other neutral class ones, not left
  out, so the course always claims the data ids (the bar part 5 fails a course with nothing on data). Unit 1's
  pretend pages may be swapped for others on the same details. Units 2 and 3 carry the step-card and block-program
  models the rest of the year and grade 4 use and are not swapped or left out. A school that leaves out a unit names
  in the course page and the unit's family note the ids not claimed that year.
- **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Unit 1's message act-out moves
  children through a cleared room at a walk, passing a paper card hand to hand; device cords stay behind the table and
  devices are carried with two hands. The scope and sequence copies these lines into the act-out and each device day.
- **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices: unit 1 $4 (printing hardware and
  software cards, system-diagram and network-diagram mats, the paper pretend pages and benefit-risk charts), unit 2 $4
  (step-card sets with choice cards, the school-grounds route sheet), unit 3 $10 (printing the paper block cards with
  if and else cards, block-and-text-view printouts, trace-table sheets and feedback forms), unit 4 $6 (data-table and
  graph sheets, survey sheets, the sorter's picture card sets), unit 5 $3 (case cards and timeline strips), about $27
  in all.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
  no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Unit 1 has online-safety and personal-information content: the
  agent checks the sharing section carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word
  (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md; APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground), that no card asks for a child's real name, screen
  name, school or street, and that no task asks what devices, games or accounts a child has at home; as background
  it reads the Federal Trade Commission's consumer advice pages on protecting kids online (consumer.ftc.gov, the page
  the agent records). Units 2 and 4 touch disability and language (a classmate who needs more time, a step-free route,
  a survey everyone can answer in more than one language and by more than one way of answering): before it reads, the
  agent studies a starting pack: the National Center on Disability and Journalism's Disability Language Style Guide
  (ncdj.org/style-guide/), the Disability Rights Education and Defense Fund's pages (dredf.org), an organization led
  by disabled people, and, for families who speak languages other than English, ColorA-n Colorado's family pages
  (colorincolorado.org); it widens the pack to the brief's five sources, two of them owned by the communities named,
  and records them. Unit 4 also touches data about people: the agent checks every survey collects no name or
  identifying detail, carries an opt-out box, and that no table is about children, homes, food or bodies. Unit 5
  touches families' work: the agent checks no child is asked about a family member's job and that the cases show many
  kinds of work and workers, rural and town. The course's checks get the answer-key agent's read. No unit is hands-on
  in the laboratory sense, so no safety agent read is planned beyond the Safety lines above, which the sensitivity
  read confirms are on each day.

