## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Software, the internet and what we share. *What tells a device what to do, how does a message reach someone far away, and what should we keep to ourselves?* Anchor: the teacher clicks "draw a circle" in a drawing tool on the class screen, a circle appears, and the class asks what did the work between the click and the circle | 6 | g3-software; g3-reach-internet; g3-share-online; g3-benefits-risks | E3-SYS-HW-12; E3-SYS-NT-14; E3-SYS-SE-13; E3-SOC-ET-17; E3-SYS-IM-15 | Concept area: systems and security, with computing and society. Practices: CT6 (say what job each piece of software does); IC3 (explain how a message gets there); ESR2 (protect information, your own and others'); ESR1 (weigh who gains and who is harmed); HCD12 (who stays informed and in control of a system they use) | Session 1 (device): software is the programs and apps that tell hardware what to do; children sort pictured things into hardware and software, name kinds of software by job (writing, drawing, a web browser for finding things, a game, a learning app), then at the device trace one task (a click on "draw a circle", a typed word) from the input, through the software, to the output, adding a software box to the system-diagram. Retrieval opening: input or output parts (grade 2). Session 2 (act-out, then one screen): a message passed from one class to another through a chain of children (devices) and a helper (the router), drawn as the network-diagram, with a labeled cloud meaning many other people's computers; then how people use different devices (a computer, a phone, a tablet) to reach the internet to find information (a search for a fact, teacher-led on one screen) and to talk with each other (a pictured video call with family far away). Session 3: on paper pretend pages, which details in a mock profile, a post or an online game could show who a person is or where they live (a school name on a shirt, a street sign in a photo, a screen name made of a real name and a town, a team name and game times), and a pop-up that says "you won a prize, type your address"; children judge what is safe to share and with whom, and rewrite one post to be safer. Retrieval opening: what happens next to a post (grade 2). Session 4: the benefit-risk-chart for a new technology in family life (a video call with relatives: seeing a grandparent; a dropped call, tired eyes, a stranger's message) and for a widely used system in the community (a map with route directions: who it helps and who it can harm, children, parents, older people, a shop owner on a quiet street, a family whose location is shared), with one thing a person can do about a harm. Out: no IP addresses, routing, packets or domain names, no network set-up or errors, no operating systems, cloud storage or version control, no catfishing or data breaches, no complex social or economic effects, no real account, real search by a child or real personal detail. End-of-unit (day 5): sort new hardware and software cards and write what the software does in a new task from input to output (E3-SYS-HW-12); label a new network-diagram and write one way people use the internet to find information and one way to talk (E3-SYS-NT-14); circle the details in a new pretend post that could show who or where, and rewrite it safer (E3-SYS-SE-13); one benefit and one risk of a new technology for a family (E3-SOC-ET-17), and who in the community a new widely used system affects and how (E3-SYS-IM-15); respond day 6. Tools: device stations (any school device with a drawing tool, job: show one input becoming one output through software); one class screen for the teacher-led search (job: show one search for a fact, nothing typed by a child). Curio Class writes: the hardware and software cards, the system-diagram and network-diagram mats, the message cards for the act-out, the paper pretend pages and pop-up, the benefit-risk charts with family and community rows | First because the system-diagram with software, the device rules and the network-diagram sit under every device day of unit 3, and the benefit-risk-chart is brought back here before unit 2 uses it to compare algorithms. Software comes before the internet so the cloud is understood as other people's computers running software; sharing follows the network it travels on; benefits and risks close the unit on the systems just met. Base: archived Year 3 "Connecting computers" lessons 4 to 6 and Year 4 "The Internet" lessons 1 to 4 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "what is the internet made of" order (networks joined into a network of networks) inside session 2, zero added days | Some homes have no device, no internet or many devices: no child is asked what is at home, which games they play or what screen name they use; examples are pictured, a child's own offer is welcome, never asked for. Sharing online: no real name, photo, school or address is said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. The map's harms are told as things that can happen to anyone, never about a classmate's family. The act-out is done at a walk in a cleared room. Devices carried with two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 2 | Algorithms that choose. *How can an algorithm make a choice, and could two ways of doing the same job be fair to different people?* Anchor: the teacher's morning step-card algorithm sends everyone out with an umbrella on a sunny day, and the class looks for the missing question | 4 | g3-algorithm-combo; g3-algorithm-effects | E3-ALG-PS-01; E3-ALG-IM-03 | Concept area: algorithms and design. Practices: CT6 (say what the algorithm must do and the question it must ask); CT8 (plan the cards before a partner follows them); ESR1 (who each way helps and who it harms); IC3 (say why, with a reason) | Session 1: a worked step-card algorithm with a choice card: a yes or no question with two paths (if it is raining, take the umbrella, else take a hat), traced by the class and then followed by a partner exactly as written; a yes or no sorting tree for classroom objects shows how each question splits a group into two paths. Retrieval opening: trace a four-card algorithm with a repeat (grade 2). Session 2 (modify): the class follows a working step-card algorithm for a tag-free playground game that combines a start card (when the whistle blows), a sequence, a repeat and a choice (if you hold the ball, pass it; else run to a cone), changes the question or one path, predicts what will be different and checks by acting it out; a partner following the cards exactly shows where a step is missing. Session 3 (make): each pair writes its own step-card algorithm for a game, a route or an act that uses a sequence, an event, a repeat and a choice across its cards, names its authors on the card set, and another pair follows it exactly and marks any missing step; this algorithm is unit 3's program. Session 4: two algorithms for one job are carried out on the class and compared by their effect on people with a wide range of needs: who uses the class computer station first (first come first served, or a time slot for each), and how to walk to the school garden (the fastest way, the safest way, a step-free way); one benefit-risk-chart per algorithm, for a classmate who needs more time, a new classmate, a classmate who uses a wheelchair, an older visitor; children write which way helps whom and who it could leave out. Out: no formal conditional logic (no and, or, not), no if inside an if, no algorithm set down as numbered sentences (grade 4) or as a flowchart or diagram with variables (grade 5), no programming in this unit, no efficiency or speed trade-offs judged at a technical level. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 11) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the morning, game and sorting-tree step-card sets with start, repeat and choice cards, the station-turn rule cards, the school-grounds route sheet with three routes, a short read-aloud about a class that changes how it shares a station | Second because unit 3's programs are built from these algorithms (g3-make-improve's model_in is the step-card made here), so the choice is understood on cards before it is a block. Comparing algorithms sits with the algorithms it judges and uses unit 1's benefit-risk-chart. After unit 1 so the station rule and the route reuse the device rules and the map just met. Base: archived Year 3 "Branching databases" lessons 3 and 4 (yes or no questions that split a group, offered on by the grade 2 frame) for session 1's sorting tree; the rest our own (Source map) | Candidate: kept, a drawing algorithm with a choice (if the side is the last one, stop; else turn) beside the game for children who cannot do the moves, zero added days | The game is played at a walk or seated, and a child may follow the cards with a toy on paper; no move is required of a child's body. Comparing algorithms for people with different needs: talk stays on the rule and the route, never on a classmate; the teacher's cards name invented people, and the wording follows what the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 3 | Programs that choose. *How do we turn our game cards into a program that makes choices, and how do we find what goes wrong?* Anchor: the class's own unit 2 game, run on the class screen as blocks, where the character runs into the cone every time because the choice asks the wrong question | 9 | g3-make-improve; g3-read-code; g3-variable-trace; g3-debug-combo | E3-PRO-PD-04; E3-PRO-PD-05; E3-PRO-RD-07; E3-PRO-VD-06; E3-PRO-TR-08 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT8 (build from the plan); ESR2 (credit who wrote the algorithm and who built it); IC5 (take and give feedback, driver and navigator roles that switch at the halfway signal); CT9 (test, debug and refine); IC3 (explain what a part does); CT6 (say what the program must do); CT7 (the variable as a named, reusable value) | Session 1 (device): a worked block program for a game that combines an event (when start is clicked), a sequence, a repeat and a choice (if touching the cone, turn around; else move forward), shown in the block-and-text-view (blocks beside their text, read, not typed), traced and predicted before it runs, then the same program with its blocks shuffled to put back in order. Retrieval opening: what does this event-and-repeat program do (grade 2). Session 2 (modify, device): change the question in the choice, the repeat count or one path, predict, run and compare. Session 3 (make, device): pairs build their own unit 2 algorithm (or a classmate's, with credit) as a block program that uses the structures it planned; the program's first comment line names who wrote the algorithm and who built it. Session 4 (device): pairs trade programs and give feedback with a look-for form (does it do what the plan says, is it clear, one suggestion), take at least one suggestion, change the program, test again and write "what I tested and what I changed"; a pair need not take every suggestion. Session 5 (device or printout): given a short program of ten lines at most in the block-and-text-view, say what one marked part is for in the whole program and what would stop working if it were taken out, tracing by pointing at each step; a worked example is read aloud first, then a partner pair reads a second. Retrieval opening: "First I ___, then I ___" for the session 3 program (grade 2). Session 6 (device or printout): a given game program with a score and a counter; children name each variable, say what is stored in it and what changes it, notice that a variable keeps one name spelled the same every time (a misspelled name makes a second box), and fill a trace-table by hand, one column per variable and one row per step; "score gets score plus 1" is read as take what is in score and put one more into it. Retrieval opening: the named box (grade 2). Session 7 (device): given programs, each with one bug, that together cover all four structures (two blocks in the wrong order, a character that does not move when clicked, a repeat that runs too many times, an if that triggers the wrong action or both paths running); trace with a trace-table where a variable is involved, find the first place it goes wrong, change one thing, test again. Out: no typed text code, no data types, no writing variable code (children identify variables in completed programs), no if inside an if or repeat inside a repeat, no functions, no multilevel project, no formal code review, no reading of error reports, no fixing more than one bug at a time; no single program is required to hold every structure, but the unit's programs hold all four. End-of-unit (day 20): a written sitting with a new short program in the block-and-text-view: say what a marked part is for and what would stop working without it (E3-PRO-RD-07); name its variables, what each stores and what changes it, and fill a four-row trace-table (E3-PRO-VD-06); find and fix the bug in a new program whose if triggers the wrong action and in a second whose repeat runs too many times, on paper (E3-PRO-TR-08); scored beside it, each pair's saved program from sessions 3 and 4 against its look-fors (built from a child-created algorithm, uses its planned structures, credit line present; E3-PRO-PD-04) and each child's own line naming the suggestion taken and the change made (E3-PRO-PD-05); respond day 21. Tools: a block editor with the block-and-text-view (blocks with icons and short words, event, repeat, if and if-else blocks, a step-through run that highlights each block and shows each variable's value, job: build, trace and debug the program the step-cards planned; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md; our own editor, STANDARD.md "Our own editor", grades 3 to 5 first). Curio Class writes: the worked, shuffled, modify, read, variable and buggy programs with their keys and the exact message the tool shows for each bug, the paper block cards with if and else cards, the feedback look-for form, the trace-table sheet | After unit 2 because g3-make-improve's model_in is the step-card algorithm the children wrote there. In the progression's in-grade order (g3-algorithm-combo notes): make and improve, which introduces the block-and-text-view, before reading a part's purpose, which reads it; then variables and the trace-table; then debugging, which uses the trace-table. Session 1 traces and predicts and session 2 modifies before the make, so no make comes before a trace or modify on the same construct (APPROACH.md order). Placed across weeks 13 to 21, which include the core check weeks 14, 16 and 20, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 4 "Repetition in games" lessons 2, 4, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "modifying a game" before "designing a game" order for sessions 2 and 3, zero added days | The credit line is credit, not blame: a helped part is praised, and feedback forms use the template's kind wording, with no comments on a person. A misspelled variable name is a program bug, never a spelling mark against the child. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Checking data and asking everyone. *Can we trust our data, and how do we collect it so everyone is counted?* Anchor: a class plant table that says one bean plant grew 400 inches in a week | 4 | g3-check-data; g3-attributes; g3-data-groups; g3-ml-new-data | E3-DAT-DC-09; E3-DAT-DI-10; E3-DAT-IM-11; E3-ALG-ML-02 | Concept area: data and analysis, with algorithms (machine learning). Practices: IC3 (say what the data shows and what it cannot); CT7 (patterns across attributes and in the examples); CT6 (plan what to collect and how); ESR1 (who a way of collecting counts or leaves out); HCD10 (include people who answer in different ways); HCD12 (an opt-out box keeps people in control); CT9 (test the sorter again after a change) | Session 1: a paper data-table with planted errors and gaps (a missing cell, a height of 400 inches, a word where a number belongs, a number where a word belongs) is checked for accuracy and completeness; children mark which entries to ask about again and which to leave out, for number and word data both. Retrieval opening: number or word data (grade 2). Session 2 (device in pairs, or one screen): a two-week class weather table with three attributes (temperature, wind, sky) answers a question about how two attributes go together (are windy days colder here?), read from a scaled picture graph (one picture stands for two days) drawn by hand and then made in a graph tool; children write what they found and one thing the data cannot tell them. Session 3: children design a short class data collection on a topic that matters to the class (which plant the class grows in the garden bed in spring, or which book series the class library adds): what to ask, whom to ask (the sources: the class, another class, the school's grown-ups), which tool (a tally chart or a paper survey with picture choices), and how every kind of student can answer (picture choices, home-language words, pointing or saying instead of writing); they compare two plans (asking only the table you sit at, or a picture survey for everyone) and say which groups each would count or leave out; the survey has an opt-out box, collects no names, and children learn they may tell a teacher or a website that they do not want to share. Retrieval opening: which way of asking leaves people out (grade 2). Session 4: the class as a sorter learns from a small training-set of insect and spider picture cards that has few spiders seen from the side, is tested on ten new cards and gets several wrong; more side-view spider examples are added, it is tested again on the same ten cards, and children tally right answers before and after and say why it changed (adding the missing kind helped); a one-screen sorting tool trained by the teacher may show the same. Retrieval opening: which new card will an uneven sorter get wrong (grade 2). Out: no statistics (no mean or median), no large data set, no more than three attributes, no required device for collecting or checking, no ethics of data collection beyond who is counted, no data about named children, no child trains a machine model, no how a model works inside; the word bias may be heard, not required. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 26) stands for it. Tools: a graph tool (enter a small table, show a scaled picture graph or bar graph, nothing stored or sent, job: show the same graph the class drew by hand); optional one-screen picture sorter (trained by the teacher in the room on picture cards, nothing stored or sent, job: show guesses before and after examples are added). Curio Class writes: the plant table with planted errors (invented for practice), the two-week weather table (invented for practice, or the class's own log, see Cross-subject), the graph sheets, the survey planning sheet with an opt-out box, the insect and spider training and test card sets, all drawn by Curio Class | After unit 3 and before the year-end check; after math grade 3's scaled graphs (see Cross-subject). Checking data comes first so the weather table is trusted before it is read; reading attributes comes before designing a collection, so children know what a good table needs; machine learning closes the unit because its training set is data that can leave a kind out, the same idea as session 3's survey. Base: archived Year 4 "Data logging" lessons 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "data for answers" step (choosing which data answers a question) inside session 2, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no food, pet, home, family or body questions; the survey topics are neutral class choices, and no answer is linked to a named child. A survey everyone can answer: talk stays on the survey's design, never on a classmate's language or ability; the wording follows what the sensitivity read settles from its pack. The sorter's mistakes are about pictures of insects and spiders, never people or faces. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
| 5 | Computing at work, then and now. *How do people use computing in their work, and why did people build the tools we use?* Anchor: a picture of a bus driver at the start of a route, a screen beside the wheel, and the question of what the screen helps with | 5 | g3-computing-at-work; g3-why-build | E3-SOC-CE-19; E3-SOC-HI-16; E3-SOC-HU-18 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: ESR1 (who each tool helped and what changed for them); HCD10 (the people each job and tool serves); HCD12 (what a tool lets people do for themselves); IC3 (say what changed and why, with a reason) | Session 1: case cards of workers in different industries (a farmer reading a weather and soil screen, a nurse checking a patient's chart on a computer, a bus driver following a route map, a builder reading plans on a tablet, a shop owner tracking what is left on the shelves), with the computing tool and skill each uses (finding information, storing data, communicating, making plans or pictures); children match worker to tool to skill and say what the job would be like without the tool. Retrieval opening: one interest and one job that uses computing (grade 2). Session 2: one everyday activity at three times on the timeline: finding a fact (a library card drawer, a library computer search, a search on a phone), keeping in touch (a letter, a phone call, a video call), paying at a shop (a written bill, a cash register, a scanned bar code); children say what people do differently now in daily life, work or talking with each other. Retrieval opening: place two tool cards on the timeline (grade 2). Session 3: why people built three computing tools: a weather forecast made on computers (to warn people before a storm), a route finder (to find the way somewhere new), and a voice helper that answers spoken questions (an AI system, built for hands-free help, also for a person who cannot easily see a screen); for each, children write the need or problem it was likely built to meet. The teacher's facts come from the Computer History Museum's online timeline (computerhistory.org/timeline/), the National Weather Service (weather.gov) for forecasts and the Bureau of Labor Statistics Occupational Outlook Handbook (bls.gov/ooh) for the worker cases, each with its "as of" date in the teacher source line, checked when the cards are written. Out: no how the tools work inside, no skills shown by children, no researching careers in depth or training needed for a job, no timeline of all of computing, no complex effects on society, no comparing tools to pick the best, no named products or companies. End-of-unit (day 31): match two new workers to a tool and a skill and explain one (E3-SOC-CE-19); put three new cards of one activity in order on a timeline and write what people do differently now (E3-SOC-HI-16); for a new pictured tool, write the need it was likely built to meet (E3-SOC-HU-18); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: the software and internet of unit 1 seen in people's work and lives, and the algorithms of unit 2 seen as things people built for a need. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 4, so it can follow the year-end check in week 26 and use weeks 28 to 32. The workers and activities do not repeat grade 2's picks (a clinic's records, a tablet drawing, game scores, a song; the abacus, khipu and braille). Our own (the partial base's Year 4 has no society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a school worker who uses computing for a job; case cards carry the row and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | No child is asked about a family member's job; a child's own offer is welcome. The cards show many kinds of work and workers, women and men, rural and town. Older ways of doing things are shown with respect, not as worse; some families still pay with written bills or keep in touch by letter. Agent reads: sensitivity. |

## Course-level checks

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Course-level check, mid-year (week 11; it stands for unit 2's end-of-unit assessment) | 2 | 1 | 1 | Day 11 scores unit 2 in full with unseen items and samples unit 1. Unit 2: from a new pictured problem (getting ready for a field day: when the bell rings, pack up, repeat for each item, if it is hot take water, else take a jacket), each child writes a step-card algorithm that uses a sequence, an event, a repeat and a choice (E3-ALG-PS-01); from two new algorithms for one job (who reads in the cozy corner: first to arrive, or a turn list), each child writes which helps whom and who it could leave out, with a reason (E3-ALG-IM-03). Unit 1 sampled with two short new items: write what the software does in a new task from input to output (E3-SYS-HW-12) and circle the details in a new pretend post that show who or where (E3-SYS-SE-13); unit 1's end-of-unit record for E3-SYS-NT-14, E3-SOC-ET-17 and E3-SYS-IM-15 is carried unscored. Written sitting of about 25 minutes, with a scribe or spoken answer for any child who needs it. Day 12, the respond day for unit 2 and the check, reteaches by error letter and gathers fresh evidence on the missed items |
| Course-level check, year-end (week 26; it stands for unit 4's end-of-unit assessment) | 4 | 1 | 1 | Day 26 scores unit 4 in full with unseen items and samples units 1 to 3. Unit 4: find the errors and the gap in a new data-table and write which entry to ask about again (E3-DAT-DC-09); from a new three-attribute table and its scaled picture graph, answer a question about how two attributes go together and write one thing the data cannot tell (E3-DAT-DI-10); for a new class topic, each child writes a short collection plan (what to ask, whom to ask, how to collect, how everyone can answer, the opt-out box) and names one group a different plan would leave out (E3-DAT-IM-11); from a new sorter's right-answer tallies before and after examples of a missing kind were added, write what changed and why (E3-ALG-ML-02). Units 1 to 3 sampled with three short new items: fill a trace-table for a new four-line program with a score (E3-PRO-VD-06), find the first wrong block in a new program whose if triggers the wrong action (E3-PRO-TR-08), and a new pretend-post item (E3-SYS-SE-13); unit 3's end-of-unit record, including each pair's program and each child's feedback line (E3-PRO-PD-04, E3-PRO-PD-05, E3-PRO-RD-07), is carried unscored. Unit 5 follows in weeks 28 to 32 and is checked by its own end-of-unit assessment. Day 27, the respond day for unit 4 and the check, reteaches and records each child's support and gains for grade 4 |

## Cross-subject

All leans are to grade 3 courses in the same year. Math, science and ELA grade 3 meet every school day, so their
course days are their school days (data/plans/calendar.csv); this course's day n falls in week n.

### Mathematics, grade 3

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest math unit that teaches it | Bridge if math teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g3-check-data | m3-scaled-graphs (3.MD.B.3: scaled picture and bar graphs) | Unit 4, session 1 (week 22) | math grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 22, weeks 1 to 5) | Met before this unit. If a school's math runs late, the session checks the table only, with no graph; the planted errors need no scale |
| g3-attributes | m3-scaled-graphs (3.MD.B.3) | Unit 4, session 2 (week 23) | math grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 22) | Met before this unit. If math runs late, the session teaches the key itself (one picture stands for two days) and counts pictures in twos with the class, with no multiplication |
| g3-variable-trace | m3-two-step-problems (3.OA.D.8: a letter standing for the unknown quantity) | Unit 3, session 6 (week 18) | math grade 3 unit 5 (days 78 to 91, weeks 16 to 19) | Math may teach it later in those weeks. The session keeps to one changing variable with a known starting value and adds of 1 or small numbers within 20, so it needs no letter for an unknown; a variable is a named box, not an unknown in an equation |

### Science, grade 3

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest science unit that teaches it | Bridge if science teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g3-attributes | s3-weather-data (3-ESS2-1: weather data in tables and graphs) | Unit 4, session 2 (week 23) | science grade 3 unit 6 (days 98 to 117, weeks 25 to 30) | Science teaches it later. Science grade 3 keeps a class weather log from the first week (its unit 6 anchor), so a class may use its own log; otherwise Curio Class supplies a two-week weather table invented for practice. The session asks only how two attributes go together in this table; what weather to expect in a season and why stays science's |
| g3-make-improve | s3-design-problem (3-5-ETS1-1: a design problem with criteria and constraints) | Unit 3, sessions 3 and 4 (weeks 15 and 16) | science grade 3 unit 2 (days 17 to 33, weeks 5 to 9) | Met before this unit. If science runs late, the feedback form's look-fors are named as "what the program must do" with no engineering words |

### English language arts, grade 3

| Row | Lean | Used here | Earliest ELA unit that teaches it | Bridge if ELA teaches it later |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| g3-make-improve | e3-writing-process (W.3.5: plan, revise and edit with help from peers and adults) | Unit 3, session 4 (week 16) | ELA grade 3 unit 1 (days 1 to 39, weeks 1 to 8) | Met before this unit. If ELA runs late, the session teaches the feedback template itself (one thing that works, one suggestion, a change made), with a teacher model first |

No row leans on social studies. Speaking, listening and short written answers (explaining an algorithm, saying what
a part of a program is for, writing a claim with a reason) are practiced here, not owned.

## For the lead

1. **After-core check weeks (calendar pass).** This course's checks sit in weeks 11 and 26, off the grade 3 core
   check weeks 9, 12, 14, 16, 20, 23, 25, 28, 30, 31 and 32. CALENDAR.md shows grade 3 week 26 already holding media
   arts', physical education's, SEL's and visual arts' year-end checks; week 11 holds no after-core check. They stay
   for the lead's one calendar pass, under the pick in the kindergarten frame's For the lead 1. If the pass needs week
   27 for the year-end check, unit 4 keeps its four sessions, the respond day moves to week 28 and unit 5 runs weeks
   29 to 32 with its respond day folded into the end-of-unit day; the lead says which.
2. **Year 4 of the partial base.** Year 4 is mapped here to grade 3, after the pilot's Year 1 to kindergarten and
   grade 2's Year 3. The Year 4 lessons were placed from their titles and unit overviews on the archived pages
   (2026-11-25), not from the lesson files; the placement is checked when the store folder is made (pilot For the
   lead 4). Taken from grade 2's offer: Year 3 "Connecting computers" lessons 4 to 6 (unit 1) and "Branching
   databases" lessons 3 and 4 (unit 2). Offered on to grade 4: Year 4 "The Internet" lessons 5 and 6 (who owns the
   web, whether to believe what we read; grade 4's g4-history-limits or the library course, which owns judging
   sources) and Year 4 "Data logging" lesson 3 (logging with a sensor, for g4-table-tool if a school has loggers).
3. **The block editor's choice blocks.** STANDARD.md "Our own editor" starts the editor with grades 3 to 5. Grade 3
   needs from it: if and if-else blocks with a yes or no question, the block-and-text-view (text shown, not typed), a
   step-through run that highlights each block and shows each variable's value, a saved comment line for the credit,
   and a print of blocks and text side by side for the paper path. The paper path meets every grade 3 id but one in
   full (item 4), so the course does not wait on it.
4. **E3-PRO-PD-04 on the no-device path (item-level pick, mine).** Its boundary places the code in a block-based
   environment, so a class with no device marks it "part met without a device", though paper block cards run by a
   partner teach the same structures. Grade 2's frame met E2-PRO-PD-04 in full on paper because its boundary counts a
   robot coded from arrows; E3's does not say so. If the lead reads paper block cards as a block-based environment,
   the mark goes and the Devices bullet says so.
5. **Device share.** About a quarter of the year's time is at a device, under APPROACH.md's "about half" for grades
   3 to 5 (ours, untested). Pick: keep it, since no E3 data, systems or society boundary asks for a device; the
   pacing line is the approach page's to change if the lead wants it per band.
6. **Source list.** docs/APPROACH-SOURCES.md, beside the pilot's and grade 2's entries: the Federal Trade Commission's
   consumer advice for kids online (consumer.ftc.gov), the Disability Rights Education and Defense Fund (dredf.org),
   ColorA-n Colorado (colorincolorado.org), the National Weather Service (weather.gov), the Bureau of Labor Statistics
   Occupational Outlook Handbook (bls.gov/ooh), and the archived Year 4 unit pages
   (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2).
7. **Row changes** (four notes cells; the lead applies them, listed in the final message): g3-why-build drops "a
   printing press" (not a computing tool) and names the frame's activity-over-time and why-built cases;
   g3-share-online gains the boundary's online-game screen name and prize pop-up; g3-ml-new-data says adding the
   missing kind is what helps; three rows' "labelled" becomes "labeled" (US spelling).

## Source map

The partial base for grade 3 is the archived edition of England's Teach Computing Curriculum, Year 4 (six units;
raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived/key-stage-2, resources dated 2023), with two Year 3 lesson ranges the grade 2
frame offered on. Placed from the public unit pages (lesson titles and unit overviews), opened 2026-11-25; each
archived Year 4 unit lists six lessons. The publisher teaches each unit's lessons in number order; where this frame
splits a unit or takes part of it, the row says why. Each adapted lesson keeps its idea, is rewritten in our words for
an American grade 3, replaces the named block language, turtle language and data loggers with our own block editor,
paper block cards and tables supplied by Curio Class, and is credited once a day. Check days are ours, never a kept
lesson.

| Base unit | Lessons | Placed in | How it is used | Left out, and why |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Year 3 Computing systems and networks: Connecting computers | 4 How am I connected?; 5 How are computers connected?; 6 What does our school network look like? | Unit 1, session 2 | Devices joined into a network and the helper that passes messages, for the act-out and the network-diagram | None of 4 to 6 (lessons 1 to 3 are grade 2's) |
| Year 4 Computing systems and networks: The Internet | 1 Connecting networks; 2 What is the internet made of?; 3 Sharing information; 4 What is a website? | Unit 1, sessions 2 and 3 | Lessons 1 and 2: networks of networks and the cloud as other people's computers (session 2); lesson 4: a website as information people reach on the internet (session 2's search); lesson 3: what is shared and who can see it (session 3) | Lessons 5 (Who owns the web?) and 6 (Can I believe what I read?): ownership of content and judging sources have no E3 id; judging sources is the library course's method (STANDARD.md section 4); offered to grade 4 |
| Year 3 Data and information: Branching databases | 3 Creating a branching database; 4 Structuring a branching database | Unit 2, session 1 | Yes or no questions that split a group into two paths, as the first picture of a choice in an algorithm | Lessons 5 and 6 (planning a branching database, a dinosaur identifier): a device database project with no E3 id beyond session 1's choice; left out (lessons 1 and 2 are grade 2's) |
| Year 4 Programming A: Repetition in shapes | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: a text-command turtle language, which the E3-PRO-PD-04 boundary keeps out of grade 3; its repeats are grade 2's; offered to no grade (grade 6 starts text, STANDARD.md "Blocks to text") |
| Year 4 Programming B: Repetition in games | 2 Different loops; 4 Modifying a game; 5 Designing a game; 6 Creating our games | Unit 3, sessions 1 to 4 | Lesson 2: a repeat that runs a set number of times and one that runs until the game stops, in the worked program (session 1); lesson 4: the modify session (session 2); lessons 5 and 6: building the class's own designed game and improving it (sessions 3 and 4), with the choice added from unit 2's cards | Lessons 1 (Using loops to create shapes) and 3 (Animate your name): repeat practice already met in grade 2 (g2-code-loops); left out |
| Year 4 Data and information: Data logging | 1 Answering questions; 2 Data collection; 4 Analysing data; 5 Data for answers; 6 Answering my question | Unit 4, sessions 1 to 3 | Lessons 1 and 2: which data answers a question and how it is collected (sessions 1 and 3); lessons 4 to 6: reading data collected over time to answer a question (session 2, the weather table) | Lesson 3 (Logging): needs a data-logging sensor, and no E3 boundary asks for one; the class reads a supplied table instead; offered to grade 4 |
| Year 4 Creating media: Audio production | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a recording app; outside bar C1's 10 percent if kept |
| Year 4 Creating media: Photo editing | Lessons 1 to 6 | None | | All six left out: no CSTA id, depends on a photo app |

Units 1 (sessions 1 and 4), 2 (sessions 2 to 4), 3 (sessions 5 to 7), 4 (session 4) and 5 are our own: the partial
base has no software-versus-hardware session, no benefits and risks of systems, no unplugged algorithm with a choice,
no comparing algorithms by their effect on people, no reading a part's purpose, no variable trace-table, no selection
debugging, no machine learning and no society unit at this year.

