247: |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
248: | 1 | Software, the internet and what we share. *What tells a device what to do, how does a message reach someone far away, and what should we keep to ourselves?* Anchor: the teacher clicks "draw a circle" in a drawing tool on the class screen, a circle appears, and the class asks what did the work between the click and the circle | 6 | g3-software; g3-reach-internet; g3-share-online; g3-benefits-risks | E3-SYS-HW-12; E3-SYS-NT-14; E3-SYS-SE-13; E3-SOC-ET-17; E3-SYS-IM-15 | Concept area: systems and security, with computing and society. Practices: CT6 (say what job each piece of software does); IC3 (explain how a message gets there); ESR2 (protect information, your own and others'); ESR1 (weigh who gains and who is harmed); HCD12 (who stays informed and in control of a system they use) | Session 1 (device): software is the programs and apps that tell hardware what to do; children sort pictured things into hardware and software, name kinds of software by job (writing, drawing, a web browser for finding things, a game, a learning app), then at the device trace one task (a click on "draw a circle", a typed word) from the input, through the software, to the output, adding a software box to the system-diagram. Retrieval opening: input or output parts (grade 2). Session 2 (act-out, then one screen): a message passed from one class to another through a chain of children (devices) and a helper (the router), drawn as the network-diagram, with a labeled cloud meaning many other people's computers; then how people use different devices (a computer, a phone, a tablet) to reach the internet to find information (a search for a fact, teacher-led on one screen) and to talk with each other (a pictured video call with family far away). Session 3: on paper pretend pages, which details in a mock profile, a post or an online game could show who a person is or where they live (a school name on a shirt, a street sign in a photo, a screen name made of a real name and a town, a team name and game times), and a pop-up that says "you won a prize, type your address"; children judge what is safe to share and with whom, and rewrite one post to be safer. Retrieval opening: what happens next to a post (grade 2). Session 4: the benefit-risk-chart for a new technology in family life (a video call with relatives: seeing a grandparent; a dropped call, tired eyes, a stranger's message) and for a widely used system in the community (a map with route directions: who it helps and who it can harm, children, parents, older people, a shop owner on a quiet street, a family whose location is shared), with one thing a person can do about a harm. Out: no IP addresses, routing, packets or domain names, no network set-up or errors, no operating systems, cloud storage or version control, no catfishing or data breaches, no complex social or economic effects, no real account, real search by a child or real personal detail. End-of-unit (day 5): sort new hardware and software cards and write what the software does in a new task from input to output (E3-SYS-HW-12); label a new network-diagram and write one way people use the internet to find information and one way to talk (E3-SYS-NT-14); circle the details in a new pretend post that could show who or where, and rewrite it safer (E3-SYS-SE-13); one benefit and one risk of a new technology for a family (E3-SOC-ET-17), and who in the community a new widely used system affects and how (E3-SYS-IM-15); respond day 6. Tools: device stations (any school device with a drawing tool, job: show one input becoming one output through software); one class screen for the teacher-led search (job: show one search for a fact, nothing typed by a child). Curio Class writes: the hardware and software cards, the system-diagram and network-diagram mats, the message cards for the act-out, the paper pretend pages and pop-up, the benefit-risk charts with family and community rows | First because the system-diagram with software, the device rules and the network-diagram sit under every device day of unit 3, and the benefit-risk-chart is brought back here before unit 2 uses it to compare algorithms. Software comes before the internet so the cloud is understood as other people's computers running software; sharing follows the network it travels on; benefits and risks close the unit on the systems just met. Base: archived Year 3 "Connecting computers" lessons 4 to 6 and Year 4 "The Internet" lessons 1 to 4 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "what is the internet made of" order (networks joined into a network of networks) inside session 2, zero added days | Some homes have no device, no internet or many devices: no child is asked what is at home, which games they play or what screen name they use; examples are pictured, a child's own offer is welcome, never asked for. Sharing online: no real name, photo, school or address is said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. The map's harms are told as things that can happen to anyone, never about a classmate's family. The act-out is done at a walk in a cleared room. Devices carried with two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
249: | 2 | Algorithms that choose. *How can an algorithm make a choice, and could two ways of doing the same job be fair to different people?* Anchor: the teacher's morning step-card algorithm sends everyone out with an umbrella on a sunny day, and the class looks for the missing question | 4 | g3-algorithm-combo; g3-algorithm-effects | E3-ALG-PS-01; E3-ALG-IM-03 | Concept area: algorithms and design. Practices: CT6 (say what the algorithm must do and the question it must ask); CT8 (plan the cards before a partner follows them); ESR1 (who each way helps and who it harms); IC3 (say why, with a reason) | Session 1: a worked step-card algorithm with a choice card: a yes or no question with two paths (if it is raining, take the umbrella, else take a hat), traced by the class and then followed by a partner exactly as written; a yes or no sorting tree for classroom objects shows how each question splits a group into two paths. Retrieval opening: trace a four-card algorithm with a repeat (grade 2). Session 2 (modify): the class follows a working step-card algorithm for a tag-free playground game that combines a start card (when the whistle blows), a sequence, a repeat and a choice (if you hold the ball, pass it; else run to a cone), changes the question or one path, predicts what will be different and checks by acting it out; a partner following the cards exactly shows where a step is missing. Session 3 (make): each pair writes its own step-card algorithm for a game, a route or an act that uses a sequence, an event, a repeat and a choice across its cards, names its authors on the card set, and another pair follows it exactly and marks any missing step; this algorithm is unit 3's program. Session 4: two algorithms for one job are carried out on the class and compared by their effect on people with a wide range of needs: who uses the class computer station first (first come first served, or a time slot for each), and how to walk to the school garden (the fastest way, the safest way, a step-free way); one benefit-risk-chart per algorithm, for a classmate who needs more time, a new classmate, a classmate who uses a wheelchair, an older visitor; children write which way helps whom and who it could leave out. Out: no formal conditional logic (no and, or, not), no if inside an if, no algorithm set down as numbered sentences (grade 4) or as a flowchart or diagram with variables (grade 5), no programming in this unit, no efficiency or speed trade-offs judged at a technical level. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 11) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the morning, game and sorting-tree step-card sets with start, repeat and choice cards, the station-turn rule cards, the school-grounds route sheet with three routes, a short read-aloud about a class that changes how it shares a station | Second because unit 3's programs are built from these algorithms (g3-make-improve's model_in is the step-card made here), so the choice is understood on cards before it is a block. Comparing algorithms sits with the algorithms it judges and uses unit 1's benefit-risk-chart. After unit 1 so the station rule and the route reuse the device rules and the map just met. Base: archived Year 3 "Branching databases" lessons 3 and 4 (yes or no questions that split a group, offered on by the grade 2 frame) for session 1's sorting tree; the rest our own (Source map) | Candidate: kept, a drawing algorithm with a choice (if the side is the last one, stop; else turn) beside the game for children who cannot do the moves, zero added days | The game is played at a walk or seated, and a child may follow the cards with a toy on paper; no move is required of a child's body. Comparing algorithms for people with different needs: talk stays on the rule and the route, never on a classmate; the teacher's cards name invented people, and the wording follows what the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |
