## Units

| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Practices and concept areas | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | When it breaks and how it connects. *When a device stops working or cannot reach the internet, how do we find out why, and how do we keep it safe?* Anchor: the class's own device station on the first day, set up by the teacher with three staged faults (the sound is off, the mouse cable is out, the wrong window is open) | 6 | g4-troubleshoot; g4-wired-wireless; g4-authn-authz; g4-resources | E4-SYS-HW-12; E4-SYS-NT-14; E4-SYS-SE-13; E4-SYS-IM-15 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: CT9 (check one thing at a time, test again); CT6 (say what is wrong before fixing it); ESR2 (proving who you are protects private information); ESR1 (who and what a device's life costs) | Session 1 (device, stations): a troubleshooting process in steps (what is wrong, is it plugged in, restart, try something simpler, ask an adult), shown first in a teacher think-aloud on one staged fault, then used by pairs on the staged faults (volume off, accessory cable out, wrong window or app open, airplane mode) until each is found and fixed; the system-diagram from grade 3 shows whether a fault sits in hardware or software. Session 2: two network-diagrams of the same room, one with cables to the router and one wireless, compared; each is better in one way (steady and fast; free to move), and the class acts out the signal along a yarn line and through the air. Session 3: a building with a front door and rooms: showing who you are at the door (a login, a password on a pretend paper account) is one thing, what you may open (a permission, a key for one room) is another; cards sorted as proving who someone is or deciding what they may do; the long words authentication and authorization given last. Session 4: a device traced on a strip from material (metals, plastics, energy in) to use to throwing away (waste out), with the benefit-risk-chart; how widely used systems affect resources and the environment, and one thing a user can do (use it longer, repair, recycle). Out: no fixes past the five steps (no settings beyond those staged, no opening a device, no installing), no network parts by name beyond device, cable, router and signal (grade 5's g5-network-parts names access point and modem), no real account or password, no confidentiality, integrity and availability (grade 5), no judging whether a device's cost is worth it. End-of-unit (day 5): put the steps in order for a new pictured fault and say the first check (E4-SYS-HW-12); for a new room, say which connection to use for two given needs and why (E4-SYS-NT-14); sort six new cards, login or permission, and explain one (E4-SYS-SE-13); trace a new device's life and name one effect and one user action (E4-SYS-IM-15); respond day 6. Tools: device stations (any school device with its own settings, job: the staged faults found and fixed). Curio Class writes: fault cards and the step cards, the two room network-diagrams, the door-and-rooms card sort and a pretend paper login, device-life strips with a dated teacher source line (the US Environmental Protection Agency's electronics donation and recycling page, epa.gov/recycle/electronics-donation-and-recycling, as of the day read) | First because the troubleshooting steps serve every device day after it (unit 3's table tool, unit 4's editor), and the year's first device session is the one with staged faults, run with the class's device rules from earlier grades. Networks, security and resources follow as the rest of the systems concept, all unplugged; resources closes the unit because it opens the benefit-risk-chart that unit 2 uses. Base: archived Year 5 "Systems and searching" lessons 1 and 2 for session 1's system-diagram retrieval (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "systems of different sizes" input, process and output sort as the retrieval inside session 1, zero added days | Staged faults only, set by the teacher (Safety); no power cords or sockets. Login and permission: pretend paper accounts, no real password said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Some homes have no device and some have many: no student is asked what is at home. Recycling: no blame on families for how they dispose of devices. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
GRADE2_SEAMS
  and checks that talk stays on the rule, never on a classmate. Unit 4 touches data about people: the agent checks
  every prompt is about class things, not children, homes, food or bodies. Unit 5 has Native and disability content
  (knotted-cord records of the Andes, a reading code for people who are blind): before it reads, the agent studies a
  starting pack: the Smithsonian National Museum of the American Indian's khipu material (americanindian.si.edu, the
  page the agent records), one Andean or Quechua community's or scholar's own account of khipu found by the agent, the
  National Federation of the Blind's pages on braille (nfb.org), an organization of blind people, and the NCDJ guide
  above. The agent widens the pack to the brief's five sources, two of them owned by those communities, and records
  them. The course's checks get the answer-key agent's read. No unit is hands-on in the laboratory sense, so no safety
  agent read is planned beyond the Safety lines above, which the sensitivity read confirms are on each day.

