CORE math-grade-3


| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Introducing multiplication. "How can we find how many without counting one by one?" | 22 | m3-scaled-graphs; m3-equal-groups | 3.MD.B.3; 3.OA.A.1 | None (math). Practices: MP4 model with mathematics; MP7 structure | Retrieve grade 2 arrays and skip-counting inside day 2, after the graph hook. Scaled picture graphs (one picture for 2, 5 or 10) and scaled bar graphs with up to six categories, drawn and read, with one- and two-step how many more and how many fewer problems; then multiplication as equal groups (5 x 7 is 5 groups of 7), from counting all to skip-counting to groups times size, with drawings, expressions and equations; arrays in rows and columns. The commutative property is met in one lesson (6 x 4 and 4 x 6) and begins m3-properties, which unit 4 owns. Multiplication problems with an unknown begin m3-mult-div-problems (unit 5 owns it), with products within 100 | The scaled graph makes each reading a small multiplication, so the year opens multiplication from data, as the base text does. Multiplication comes first because it is the grade's largest major work and every later unit uses it | Candidate: Progressions MD (scaled graphs, PDF 85) for the six-category limit and the two-step comparison problems. Extension: a scaled bar graph with a scale of 5 or 10 that the student chooses and defends | Survey data comes from class choices (favorite fruit, games), never from family income, body size or home life. Supplies: counters, grid paper |
| 2 | Area and multiplication. "How much space does a shape cover, and how can multiplying tell us?" | 16 | m3-area-concepts; m3-area-multiply | 3.MD.C.5a; 3.MD.C.5b; 3.MD.C.6; 3.MD.C.7a; 3.MD.C.7b; 3.MD.C.7d | None (math). Practices: MP6 precision (units of area); MP7 structure | Area as how much a flat shape covers: a unit square, tiling with no gaps or overlaps (3.MD.C.5a, b), counting square centimeters, meters, inches, feet and made-up units (3.MD.C.6); tiles seen as rows of an array, so the count equals the product of the sides (3.MD.C.7a); multiplying side lengths in problems and showing products as rectangles (3.MD.C.7b); areas of rectilinear figures cut into rectangles and added (3.MD.C.7d). The area model (a rectangle labeled with its side lengths) is introduced here. 3.MD.C.7c waits for unit 4 | Area needs only unit 1's multiplication and array, and gives the area model that units 4 and 6 use. The base text puts it second | Candidate: Progressions GM (area, PDF 95 and 107): rows of tiles as composite units, and the error of counting tile corners. Extension: the area of a room plan made of three rectangles, found two ways | Supplies: square tiles or paper squares, grid paper in centimeters and inches, rulers |
| 3 | Adding and subtracting within 1,000. "Which way to add or subtract is best for these numbers?" | 21 | m3-add-sub-1000; m3-round | 3.NBT.A.2; 3.NBT.A.1 | None (math). Practices: MP3 compare and critique methods; MP6 precision | Retrieve grade 2 adding and subtracting within 1,000 and place value inside day 39. Fluent adding and subtracting within 1,000 with strategies and with written methods based on place value, recorded with base-ten drawings, open number lines and the place value chart (introduced here), compared and explained; the standard algorithm is not required (grade 4). Rounding whole numbers within 1,000 to the nearest 10 or 100 with place value and a number line: three-digit numbers to the hundred and two-digit numbers to the ten stressed, and three-digit numbers to the ten also taught and checked (3.NBT.A.1 in full); no fixed rule for a number exactly halfway. Lessons 2 and 5 begin m3-arithmetic-patterns (owned by unit 4) with patterns in a partly filled addition table, explained with the properties. The last four lessons begin two-step problems (m3-two-step-problems, owned by unit 5) with addition and subtraction and rounding to check | Grade 3's add and subtract is a maintained fluency (Progressions NBT), placed early so the year's problems can use numbers to 1,000, and before unit 5's two-step problems, which need rounding to check answers | Candidate: Progressions NBT (PDF 68): the halfway note on rounding, and methods that grow to larger numbers. Extension: every three-digit number that rounds to 500 to the nearest hundred and to 450 to the nearest ten | Supplies: base-ten blocks or drawings, place value chart on paper. The base text's subtraction-algorithm lessons are merged (source map) to give time back to major work |
| 4 | Division and the multiplication table. "How are multiplying and dividing related?" | 16 | m3-division-meaning; m3-properties; m3-area-distributive; m3-arithmetic-patterns; m3-mult-fluency | 3.OA.A.2; 3.OA.B.6; 3.OA.B.5; 3.MD.C.7c; 3.OA.D.9; 3.OA.C.7 | None (math). Practices: MP7 structure; MP8 repeated reasoning | Division with its two meanings (how many in each share, how many groups) with objects, drawings and arrays, no remainders (3.OA.A.2); division as an unknown factor (3.OA.B.6). The properties as strategies without their formal names: the order of factors (reviewed from unit 1), grouping factors (3 x 5 x 2 as 3 x 10, a day of our own), breaking a factor apart, shown with arrays and area models (3.OA.B.5); tiling a rectangle of sides a and b + c to show a x b + a x c (3.MD.C.7c, which completes 3.MD.C.7 with unit 2). Patterns in the multiplication table, explained with the properties, and the addition-table patterns of unit 3 recalled beside them (3.OA.D.9, finished here). Fact strategies (related facts, doubling, breaking apart, the nines), the related division facts, and wider mixed facts practice (begun in unit 1), with two days of our own on strategies and an untimed private facts record (3.OA.C.7) | Division is the inverse of the multiplication of units 1 and 2, and the area pictures of unit 2 are what show the break-apart strategy. Fact strategies are taught here so the facts practiced since unit 1 have the rest of the year to grow into memory | Candidate: Progressions OA (PDF 37 to 38): Table 3's two division types, and the note that fluency takes time and support. Extension: every way to split 7 x 8 into two known facts, and which is quickest | Fact practice is spaced and private; no timer shown, no posted scores. Supplies: counters, grid paper, multiplication table on paper |
| 5 | Multiplying and dividing larger numbers. "How can facts we know help with bigger numbers and harder problems?" | 14 | m3-multiples-of-ten; m3-mult-div-problems; m3-two-step-problems | 3.NBT.A.3; 3.OA.A.3; 3.OA.A.4; 3.OA.D.8 | None (math). Practices: MP1 make sense of problems; MP4 model with mathematics | One-digit numbers times multiples of 10 from 10 to 90 by thinking in tens (9 x 8 tens is 72 tens) (3.NBT.A.3); products of teen numbers and numbers past 20 broken apart with the unit 4 properties (review); one-step multiplication and division problems within 100 with equal groups, arrays and measurement quantities (a day of our own on lengths, masses and liquid volumes), the unknown in every position (3.OA.A.3), and unknowns in equations such as 8 x ? = 48 (3.OA.A.4); two-step problems with all four operations, written as equations with a letter for the unknown, checked with mental math and rounding (3.OA.D.8, which unit 3 began), with the conventional order when there are no parentheses (a day of our own) | It needs division (unit 4), the properties (unit 4) and rounding (unit 3). The two-step standard is finished here, where all four operations are available | Candidate: Progressions OA (PDF 34 and 37): all three unknown positions for equal groups, arrays and measurement, spread over units 1 and 5. Extension: a two-step problem written for a partner, with its equation and a letter for the unknown | Story contexts (sharing, garden rows, bus seats) use a range of names. Supplies: base-ten drawings, tape diagram paper |
| 6 | Fractions as numbers. "Where does a fraction live on the number line?" | 23 | m3-partition-shapes; m3-unit-fractions; m3-fractions-number-line; m3-equivalent-fractions; m3-compare-fractions | 3.G.A.2; 3.NF.A.1; 3.NF.A.2a; 3.NF.A.2b; 3.NF.A.3a; 3.NF.A.3b; 3.NF.A.3c; 3.NF.A.3d | None (math). Practices: MP3 justify comparisons; MP5 use tools (strips, number lines) | Retrieve grade 2 halves, thirds and fourths inside day 92. Shapes cut into parts of equal area, each a unit fraction, equal parts not always the same shape (3.G.A.2, with a day of our own on equal-area parts of different shapes); 1/b as one of b equal parts and a/b as a parts of size 1/b, with the whole mostly a length (3.NF.A.1; the fraction strip introduced); fractions on the number line: 1/b from 0 (3.NF.A.2a), a/b as a lengths of 1/b (3.NF.A.2b), fractions greater than 1 on lines past 1 (a day of our own); equivalent fractions as the same size or the same point (3.NF.A.3a), made and explained (3.NF.A.3b), whole numbers as fractions (3 = 3/1, 4/4 = 1) (3.NF.A.3c); comparing fractions with the same numerator or denominator, the same whole, with >, = and < (3.NF.A.3d, the expected error that a larger denominator means a larger fraction named in advance). Denominators 2, 3, 4, 6 and 8 only | The grade's second major idea, placed after the whole-number multiplication it does not need, so the year's middle goes to it, as in the base text. It uses the number line of unit 3 and the area model of unit 2 | Candidate: Progressions NF (PDF 146 to 148): the length model first, and comparisons only for the same whole. Extension: fractions greater than 1 in halves, fourths and eighths placed on one number line | Food contexts (pizza, sandwiches) are fine; avoid diet talk. Supplies: paper fraction strips, number line paper |
| 7 | Measuring length, time, liquid volume and mass. "How do we measure, and how do we solve problems with measurements?" | 17 | m3-line-plots; m3-mass-volume; m3-time | 3.MD.B.4; 3.MD.A.2; 3.MD.A.1 | None (math). Practices: MP2 reason abstractly and quantitatively (base lesson 6.12, measurement situations shown as tape diagrams and equations, is the anchor task); MP5 use tools (rulers, scales, clocks) | Retrieve grade 2 measuring, line plots and telling time at their uses inside days 117, 120 and 125. Measuring lengths to the half and quarter inch to make data, shown on a line plot scaled in wholes, halves or quarters (3.MD.B.4); estimating and measuring masses in grams and kilograms and liquid volumes in liters, reading scales as vertical number lines, and one-step problems with all four operations in the same units (3.MD.A.2); time to the nearest minute, intervals in minutes, and adding and subtracting intervals on a number line marked in minutes (3.MD.A.1). No converting units (grade 4) | Line plots need the fractions of unit 6 (halves and fourths on a number line). Measurement problems use every operation the year has taught. The base text's order | Candidate: Progressions GM (PDF 108 to 109): liquid volume read on a vertical scale, and elapsed time on a number line. Extension: a class schedule of four events with the elapsed minutes found on a number line | Teacher note from the row: water and balances at stations need a mop and dry hands near any electrical device. No student measures or reports body weight. Supplies: rulers in halves and fourths of an inch, balance or kitchen scale, liter containers, a clock |
| 8 | Shapes and perimeter. "What makes a shape belong to a group, and how far is it around?" | 15 | m3-quadrilaterals; m3-perimeter | 3.G.A.1; 3.MD.D.8 | None (math). Practices: MP3 define and argue about categories; MP7 structure | Retrieve grade 2 shape attributes on day 134 and length problems again on day 139. Shapes in different categories share attributes, and a shared attribute defines a larger category: rhombuses, rectangles and squares are quadrilaterals; drawing quadrilaterals that are none of these (3.G.A.1; the shape-hierarchy diagram introduced). Perimeter of polygons from side lengths, an unknown side, and rectangles with the same perimeter and different areas or the same area and different perimeters (3.MD.D.8), with the expected error of mixing up perimeter and area named in advance | Perimeter comes after area (unit 2) so the two can be compared, and the shape categories set up grade 4's classifying. Supporting and additional work last, as in the base text, so the major work has the year's best weeks | Candidate: Progressions G (PDF 130): categories by sides and right angles only, no measured angles. Extension: every rectangle with whole-number sides and a perimeter of 24 units, ordered by area | The term trapezoid is not needed in grade 3; if it comes up, the course uses the inclusive definition (at least one pair of parallel sides) that grades 4 and 5 use. Supplies: geoboards or dot paper, rulers, string |


CORE science-grade-3


| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Pushes, pulls and swings. "What makes a swing start, stop and keep coming back?" Anchor: a playground swing (and a class pendulum of string and washers) that one push sets going, that swings in a pattern we can predict, and that two equal pushes from opposite sides hold still | 16 | s3-balanced-forces; s3-motion-patterns | 3-PS2-1; 3-PS2-2 | Practices: planning and carrying out investigations (a fair test, changing one thing at a time; observations and measurements). Crosscutting: cause and effect; patterns | Pushes and pulls have a strength and a direction; balanced forces (equal pushes on a box from both sides) give no change in motion and an unbalanced force starts, stops, speeds up or turns an object; gravity named as a pull toward the ground. The force arrow drawing, revised as tests come in. A fair test, first as a class and then in small groups: how many pushes, how strong compared with another, which direction, one change at a time. Motion that repeats (the swing, a pendulum, a ball rolling back and forth in a bowl, a see-saw) recorded in a table so the pattern shows; the pattern used to predict where the object will be next, and a failed prediction read as a sign something changed. The weather log starts in week 1 | Forces open the year because the fair test taught here (the first of grades 3 to 5, building on the fair comparisons of grades 1 and 2) is used in every later unit, and the force arrows are the year's first new model, needed in unit 2. Motion patterns sit beside balanced forces on the same swing, so one anchor drives both rows | Candidate: dropped, the come-back can, zero days; the bowl motion on day 11 supplies a second pattern without elastic construction | Readiness on kindergarten's pushes and pulls and grade 1 prediction in day 1's last ten minutes, after the hook; day 2 support. End-of-unit performances split: day 14 repeating-motion record/prediction, day 15 force investigation; response day 16 only. Swings: no student on a swing during an investigation; the class pendulum is the tested object. Boundary: force size is compared, never measured in units; no period or frequency. Lengths measured to the whole inch or centimetre until math teaches halves and quarters. Simulation to list: a pendulum and balanced-push tool for the no-kit path. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 2 | Pushes and pulls without touching. "How can things push and pull without touching, and how can we put that to work?" Anchor: a steel paper clip on a thread that hangs in the air, leaning toward a magnet it never touches, and a balloon rubbed on hair that lifts bits of paper | 17 | s3-magnet-static; s3-design-problem | 3-PS2-3; 3-PS2-4; 3-5-ETS1-1 | Practices: asking questions and defining problems (testable questions; a design problem defined by criteria and constraints). Crosscutting: cause and effect; how science, engineering and technology work together | Students ask testable questions about pulls and pushes between objects that do not touch and investigate them: magnet to magnet, magnet to paper clips, one magnet against two, distance, and turning a magnet around (attract becomes repel); static electricity with a balloon on hair and paper bits. Force arrows drawn between objects that are not touching. Then a design problem a magnet can solve, written as need, criteria and constraints (materials, time, cost) precisely enough that two solutions could be judged against it; a brief comparison shows how a changed need (the cupboard now opened by a younger class) changes the criteria, the 3-5-ETS1-1 element on changing human needs and wants. The clarification's electromagnet example as a teacher demonstration: a nail wound with wire on one D cell picks up paper clips, switched on and off; students observe and ask questions, and build no circuit (circuits are grade 4). The named problem: a magnetic latch that keeps the class supply cupboard door shut but opens with one hand; the second clarification example (keeping two moving toy cars from touching) is the transfer item. Students may sketch and try one solution | Needs the force arrows of unit 1. The design problem follows the magnet investigations because its criteria come from what students found (how distance changes the pull). It sits early so the criteria language is in place before units 5 and 6 judge solutions | Candidate: dropped, compass extension, zero days; retain inquiry time for electric and magnetic interactions and full problem definition | Small and strong magnets are a swallowing hazard and harm devices and cards: count magnets out and back; no magnets near phones or tablets. Static electricity only for students; no student circuits (the electromagnet is the teacher's demonstration, wire ends taped, the cell disconnected between uses because the wire warms). Planting day falls in this unit's first week (science day 18, see the brief): about 15 minutes for unit 3's pots and mealworms. Comparing many solutions is grade 4 and fair tests of a prototype are grade 5: this unit defines the problem in full. The latch's holding criterion is counted in washers held, not grams, until math teaches mass. Retrieval task on kindergarten's pushes and pulls (s3-magnet-static's handoff). Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 3 | Growing up and taking after. "How do living things grow and change, and why do they look like their parents but not exactly?" Anchor: the class's own plants, grown from one packet of seeds sown on planting day (science day 18): they come up looking like the picture of the parent plant but not quite like each other; and photographs of one kind of hydrangea bush with blue flowers in one garden and pink in another (the soil decides the color) | 22 | s3-life-cycles; s3-inherited-traits; s3-environment-traits | 3-LS1-1; 3-LS3-1; 3-LS3-2 | Practices: developing and using models (life-cycle models); analyzing and interpreting data (traits); constructing explanations (environment). Crosscutting: patterns; cause and effect | Life cycles watched first-hand (the fast-cycling plants or radishes sown on planting day, and mealworms becoming darkling beetles) and modeled as drawn cycles and sequenced cards; compared with other organisms' cycles (a butterfly, a frog, a flowering plant) to find that every cycle differs in stages and timing yet all share birth, growth, reproduction and death; the model revised when observation shows a missed stage. Traits: photos, tallies and bar graphs of puppies in a litter, seedlings from one packet and ear length in rabbits show offspring are like their parents and siblings vary. Then the environment, opened by one retrieval task on grade 2's light-and-water test: the class crowding test (the same seeds sown close together and spread out, the rest kept the same; crowded plants stay smaller) and other cases (the hydrangea whose soil decides its flower color, plants stunted by too little water, a dog fed too much and walked too little, flamingos fed less shrimp turning paler) support the explanation that the environment can change a trait; students sort traits into inherited, shaped by the environment, or both | The plants and mealworms are set up on planting day in unit 2 so the life cycle can be watched inside this unit. Life cycles come first because reproduction makes offspring whose traits the next lesson compares; inherited traits come before environment traits (the row's own order) so students can sort the two. Before unit 4, which needs living things' traits as variation | Candidate: kept, seed-packet timing, 15 minutes inside day 42; harvest is distinguished from reproduction | Living things handled humanely; mealworms and plants kept, returned or released only as local rules allow; check allergies before soil, plants or insects. Non-human examples only for traits: no student is asked about family resemblance or adoption. No people's weight or bodies as environment examples. Boundary: flowering plants only; no human reproduction; no genes; no soil chemistry for the hydrangea (the soil differs, the color follows). Readiness on grade 1's like-parents in day 36's last ten minutes; support day 38. Retrieve grade 2's plant test once, do not rerun it. End tasks: environmental explanation day 55, cycle models day 56, inherited-trait data day 57; respond day 58 once. Plant heights in whole centimetres until math teaches halves and quarters of an inch. Simulation to list: a time-lapse plant-growth viewer for the no-kit path. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 4 | Surviving where you live. "Why do some living things survive in a place while others do not?" Anchor: the Arctic tundra in winter: musk oxen form a ring against wolves, white-coated hares are hard to see on the snow, and no rainforest tree frog or cactus could live there | 20 | s3-animal-groups; s3-variation-advantage; s3-habitat-survival | 3-LS2-1; 3-LS4-2; 3-LS4-3 | Practices: engaging in argument from evidence (groups; habitats); constructing explanations (variation). Crosscutting: cause and effect; systems and system models; structure and function | An argument with claim, evidence and reasoning that some animals form groups that help members survive: wolves hunt in packs, fish school, bees divide work, musk oxen circle; evidence from video, hunting success given as counts out of 10 hunts alone and in a group read from a bar graph (the unit plan takes the counts from its source; no percent), and readings; the argument admits that some animals live alone and survive in other ways. Variation: the class camouflage hunt (paper "moths" of several colors on a patterned cloth, found by "bird" classmates) gives data that the better-hidden survive more often; plants with bigger thorns are eaten less; a sourced case in one kind of bird whose brighter-colored males attract mates more often (the mates case, with the data students explain; no claim that brighter animals always do); a small difference changes who survives, finds mates and has young. Habitat: in a given habitat some organisms survive well, some less well and some not at all (a cactus in a desert and in a bog; in the Arctic, the polar bear survives well, the wood frog survives partial freezing in winter and thaws in spring, and a rainforest tree frog could not survive at all; each judged from evidence of the organism's needs and characteristics and the place's conditions, never from the animal's group, and the wood frog not labeled as surviving less well just because its trait is special; the wood frog links back to ELA grade 3's frog unit), argued from the organisms' needs and the habitat's conditions, with organisms and habitat as one system | Needs unit 3's idea that individuals of one kind vary. Groups, variation and habitat answer one question about one place, so the tundra anchor drives all three rows. Comes before unit 5, which changes the environment and asks who survives then | Candidate: kept, local habitat, day 73 plus ten minutes on day 72; photograph inventory when access or weather prevents outdoor work | The camouflage hunt is a game about paper shapes, not a role play of predator and prey with children as victims. Hunting shown without graphic images. Boundary: no natural selection by name, no genetics, no animal behavior mechanisms. Retrieve grade 1 parent-offspring survival behavior on day 60 (last used in grade 2; no readiness check on day 58); retrieve parts-mimic, used explicitly in grade 2's seed model. End tasks: variation explanation day 75, group argument day 76, habitat argument day 77; respond day 78 once. Science teaches its own claim, evidence and reasoning frame here. Simulation to list: the camouflage hunt as a no-kit tool. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 5 | Environments change. "How do we know places have changed, and what happens to living things when they do?" Anchor: fossil tree stumps of an early kind of tree, preserved where they grew in rock in the Arctic on Svalbard, Norway, from a time when that land lay near the equator; no such forest grows there now (a teacher may swap in a fossil found in the class's own state); the sea fossil on a mountain is grade 6 unit 4's anchor, so it is not used here | 16 | s3-fossils; s3-environment-change-solution | 3-LS4-1; 3-LS4-4 | Practices: analyzing and interpreting data (fossil finds); engaging in argument from evidence (merit of a solution). Crosscutting: scale, proportion and quantity (deep time); systems and system models; stability and change | Fossils by kind, size and where they are found as evidence of past organisms and environments: sea shells on dry land show sea once covered it (worked case: the fossil whales in the desert of Wadi Al-Hitan, Egypt), tropical plant fossils in the Arctic show a once-warm climate, fossils of extinct organisms show living things have changed; older fossils lie deeper in undisturbed layers, so ages are relative. Nature of science: explanations of the past come from evidence and change when new evidence arrives. Then the present: when an environment changes (a new highway through a forest, a dam on a river, a fire) some living things survive, some move and some die; students make a claim about the merit of a solution judged against the criteria language of unit 2. Worked case: a wildlife crossing over a highway; second case: a fish ladder at a dam; transfer: replanting after a fire. Two short cases complete the clarification's kinds: a stream warmed when the shading trees on its bank are cut (temperature) and a new plant that spreads and crowds out the food of local animals (other organisms); with the highway (land), the dam (water) and the fire (food), each kind has a case, one change at a time | Needs unit 4's habitat as a system (who survives depends on the conditions) and unit 2's criteria. Fossils and present-day change answer the same question at two time scales, so they share a unit; it comes before unit 6, which makes the unit-2 criteria do design work on weather | Candidate: kept, state or documented regional fossil, 20 minutes inside day 86; infer the environment, no naming exercise | BASELINE rule 5: Earth's long history and change in living things are stated as the evidence shows, without softening; a family question is answered with what the evidence shows. Boundary: no named fossils or species to memorize; relative age only; one environmental change at a time; climate change not assessed here, answered plainly if raised. Readiness check on kindergarten's reduce-impact (a one-lesson reteach inside the unit's days). Real fossils or casts optional; the no-kit path uses public-domain photographs of finds. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |
| 6 | Weather, climate and hazards. "What weather can we expect here, and how can people get ready for the dangerous kind?" Anchor: the class's own weather log since the first week: no two days alike, yet each season has its own kind of weather, and, this year or last, the town had a weather warning (from the National Weather Service's record for the county if the class has not seen one) | 20 | s3-weather-data; s3-climates; s3-weather-hazard | 3-ESS2-1; 3-ESS2-2; 3-ESS3-1 | Practices: analyzing and interpreting data (weather); obtaining, evaluating and communicating information (climates); engaging in argument from evidence (merit of a design). Crosscutting: patterns; cause and effect | The class log and local records in tables, pictographs and bar graphs: monthly average temperature, rain or snow, and wind direction, to describe the typical weather of each season; averages are supplied, not calculated; one day's weather is not a season's. Climate (the pattern over many years) told from weather (today), from texts, maps and data: warm near the equator and cold near the poles, wet and dry places; the named comparison: our town, a rainforest city near the equator (Manaus, Brazil), a desert city (Cairo, Egypt) and a polar town (Utqiagvik, Alaska). Then a claim about the merit of designs that reduce a weather hazard's impact, judged against criteria and constraints: the three the standard names (a barrier against flooding, a wind-resistant roof, a lightning rod), with a quick model test of a roof that stays on under a fan as the class's evidence. People cannot stop severe weather but can lessen its harm | Last, so the weather log holds most of a year and the math course has taught scaled bar graphs. Climates follow weather data (the row's order: typical conditions known first). The hazard design uses unit 2's criteria and unit 5's practice of judging a solution's merit | Candidate: kept, dated, located public weather-service forecast and warning in our own words, 15 minutes inside day 108 | Some students may have lived through floods, fires or storms: examples kept general, no student asked to recount one, sharing is the student's choice. Fan tests: low setting, fingers clear, eye protection if anything can fly. Climates are compared in words, maps and counts (months below freezing, months with rain); no temperature below zero is graphed or compared (negative numbers are grade 6). Boundary: pictographs and bar graphs only; no climate causes beyond the pattern; climate change not assessed, answered plainly if raised. Retrieval task on kindergarten's forecasts (kept in use in grade 2); the weather-watch habit needs none, as the class log has run all year. Simulation to list: a weather-data viewer of public records for classes without a log. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (hands-on). |


CORE english-language-arts-grade-3


| # | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | C3 | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Reading against the odds. "How do people get past what keeps them from books and school?" | 39 | e3-text-questions; e3-central-message; e3-character-actions; e3-pictures-and-words; e3-reading-range; e3-main-idea; e3-literal-nonliteral; e3-word-strategies; e3-long-words; e3-fluency; e3-informative; e3-writing-tasks; e3-writing-process; e3-discussion; e3-conventions | RL.3.1; RI.3.1; RL.3.2; RL.3.3; RL.3.7; RI.3.7; RL.3.10; RI.3.10; RI.3.2; SL.3.2; RL.3.4; L.3.5; L.3.4; RF.3.3; RF.3.4; SL.3.5; W.3.2; W.3.4; W.3.10; W.3.5; W.3.6; SL.3.1; SL.3.3; L.3.2 | Not used (ELA). Leans on ss3-map-skills | Anchors: three original Curio Class picture-book stories set today, each in a different place, of children who work to reach books and school (a girl whose library comes by boat; a boy who walks to a once-a-week book bus; a girl who learns to read beside her grandmother), 300 to 800 words, measure targets about 420 to 620; a short set of fables and folktales of learning from named cultures (300 to 600 words); an informational set on how books reach children (a paired original article on book carriers today and an anchor article on the 1930s packhorse librarians of Kentucky, built on public-domain federal records and photographs); a short Curio Class retelling of how Booker T. Washington learned to read, with a few lines from his own 1901 account, opened by one or two plain sentences on what slavery and freedom meant for him. Asking and answering questions with the place in the text named (RL.3.1, RI.3.1); recounting each story and stating its message with the details that carry it (RL.3.2, fables and folktales here, myths in unit 2); describing a character by traits tied to actions and how the actions drive events (RL.3.3); what illustrations add to mood and character, and placing each story on a world map from its pictures and words (RL.3.7, RI.3.7); main idea and key details of the informational set, of a text read aloud, and of a short recorded library presentation with a labeled bar graph of books lent each month (numbers within grade 2 math), told from the words, the picture and the numbers together (RI.3.2, SL.3.2); words and phrases as used in the stories, literal and nonliteral meaning told apart (RL.3.4, L.3.5a; real-life connections, 5b, and shades of meaning, 5c, continued on the unit 2 poems); a strategy chosen for each unknown word: context, a known affix, a known root, a glossary or dictionary (L.3.4a to d); the independent reading system launched (log, conference, book choice). Writing: the daily exit ticket (W.3.10) and an informative piece, one paragraph growing to two, on why getting books is hard in some places, with grouped facts, linking words and a conclusion (W.3.2a to d, W.3.4); a reading contract drafted, critiqued against one look-for and revised with peer and teacher feedback, and the reading-strategy bookmark planned and published (W.3.5: planning, revising and editing taught here; using technology to produce and publish, W.3.6, waits for keyboarding in unit 2). Fluency: a passage from a unit 1 story reread for accuracy, rate and expression and confirmed in context (RF.3.4a to c), then recorded as audio read for listeners (SL.3.5); poetry read aloud is added in unit 2. Talk: the discussion routine and norms (SL.3.1a to d), asking a speaker questions (SL.3.3), speaking in complete sentences when the discussion asks for detail (SL.3.6 in its grade 2 form; owned in unit 3). Skills block from day 1: the long-word screen, then the sequence (RF.3.3a prefixes and derivational suffixes with meanings first; RF.3.3b Latin suffixes from unit 2, RF.3.3c multisyllable words from unit 3, RF.3.3d the year's irregular words all year); spelling on the same patterns, possessives (L.3.2d), suffix spelling and dictionary checks (L.3.2e to g); dialogue punctuation (2c) comes in unit 2, titles (2a) in unit 3, commas in addresses (2b) in unit 4. Retrieval: grade 2's wh-questions and simple and compound sentences; the fall oral reading screen is diagnostic (warm-ups). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.3.2a (unit 3); L.3.2b (unit 4); L.3.2c, L.3.5b, L.3.5c (unit 2). | The base text's module 1, kept whole: it opens the year with close reading, the discussion routine, independent reading and the exit ticket, which every later unit uses, and it already teaches and assesses word meaning in the stories (RL.3.4, L.3.4), the recorded reading (RF.3.4, SL.3.5) and the revised reading contract (W.3.5), so those rows sit here. Short whole texts come before the long chapter book of unit 3. Two days are added for the start-of-year screens (long words, oral reading, a writing sample) | Candidate: rubric 1g to 1n (the long-word sequence taught in order, with checks and next steps), 1t and 1u (fluency practice with checks: the recorded reading), 2i (the discussion routine written with teacher guidance), 2e (a text set whose children and places vary) | Children kept from school by poverty, distance or work: framed through their effort, not the danger; no war or violence shown. The Booker T. Washington lines touch slavery: named plainly, people named as enslaved people, the excerpt short and previewed by the teacher, with its own short context (social studies teaches slavery later, in its unit 5; the task asks about the selection, not prior history). No student is asked to share family reading struggles or literacy. Each place is real and named, never a generic "poor country". Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |
| 2 | Frogs and the places they live. "How does where a frog lives shape how it looks and acts?" | 38 | e3-text-connections; e3-text-features; e3-academic-words; e3-narrative; e3-short-research; e3-word-forms; e3-sentences | RI.3.3; RI.3.8; RI.3.5; RI.3.4; L.3.6; W.3.3; L.3.3; W.3.7; W.3.8; L.3.1b; L.3.1c; L.3.1d; L.3.1e; L.3.1g; L.3.1 | Not used (ELA). Leans on science grade 3 (life cycles, traits and habitats) | Anchors: original Curio Class frog poems and public-domain animal poems; two pourquoi tales from the base text (its own CC BY tales of why the frog has a long tongue and why the poison dart frog is so colorful, adapted) and short myths from named cultures; an original informational book on frogs (life cycle, adaptations, habitats, 400 to 900 words a section with headings, measure target about 600 to 820), and short federal wildlife pages on three unusual frogs (a glass frog, a horned frog, a water-holding frog). A frog's life cycle in order and why its skin or color helps it live, with time, sequence and cause words, and how sentences and paragraphs link (RI.3.3, RI.3.8); finding answers to the class's own frog questions with headings, sidebars, key words and an index, and on a teacher-chosen list of web pages (RI.3.5); general academic and domain words named as two kinds, with words of space and time (RI.3.4, L.3.6). Writing: a pourquoi tale ("why do tadpoles wiggle?") with a narrator, dialogue and time words, revised for word choice (W.3.3a to d, L.3.3a, with dialogue punctuation L.3.2c); expert-group research on one unusual frog, notes sorted under body, habitat and how it survives (W.3.7, W.3.8); an informative essay built to three linked paragraphs, planned, critiqued, typed and published as a page of a class frog book (W.3.2 and W.3.5 used again; keyboarding and publishing with technology begin here, W.3.6 from unit 1's e3-writing-process). Grammar taught on sentences from the frog texts and the students' drafts: regular and irregular verbs (L.3.1d), simple past, present and future tense (1e), comparative and superlative adjectives and adverbs (1g, "the smallest frog", "jumps farther"), coordinating and subordinating conjunctions (L.3.1h) and simple, compound and complex sentences combined from the frog texts (L.3.1i); plurals (1b) and abstract nouns (1c) are continued in unit 4, the function of parts of speech (1a) and agreement (1f) in unit 3. Review and extension: e3-literal-nonliteral on the poems and tales, now with real-life connections and shades of meaning for knowing and wondering (L.3.5b and c); e3-fluency on poems read aloud and a poem recording (RF.3.4b, SL.3.5); e3-word-strategies on the frog words; e3-main-idea and e3-central-message (myths here) on the tales and articles. Two frog texts read side by side without the comparison task (grade 2's comparison, in review; RI.3.9 is taught in unit 4). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. Lettered parts of these standards taught in another unit: L.3.1a, L.3.1f (unit 3); L.3.3b (unit 3, unit 4). | The base text's module 2: research needs unit 1's routines and main idea, and the frog book is the year's first piece typed and published. The base text teaches and assesses verbs, tenses, comparatives and complex sentences on the frog writing, so e3-word-forms and e3-sentences sit here. Science leans same year (life cycles, traits) | Candidate: rubric 2s and 2t (research taught, then used), 2q (planning, revising and publishing with technology), 2m and 2n (sentence work from the anchor, then in the student's own editing) | Animals eating and being eaten described plainly, not graphically. Web research on a teacher-chosen list only; no student account. Myths credited to the people who tell them. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
| 3 | A chapter-book series. "Would you recommend this series to a friend, and why?" | 39 | e3-story-parts; e3-point-of-view; e3-same-author; e3-opinion; e3-speaking-reports | RL.3.5; RL.3.6; RI.3.6; RL.3.9; W.3.1; SL.3.4; SL.3.6; L.3.3b | Not used (ELA) | Anchors: book 1 of an original Curio Class chapter-book series set today (about 10,000 to 20,000 words, measure target about 620 to 820), read whole as a class; book 2 of the same series, about the same characters, read in part (at least three connected chapters, enough to compare theme, setting and plot) or as the independent book; a two-scene play script from book 1; a short informational anchor on how series books are made (g3-u3-04). Chapter, scene and stanza used when talking and writing, and how each part builds on the ones before (RL.3.5): what each chapter adds to the ones before, followed on the narrative arc; what the second scene of the script adds to the first; and, returning to one unit 2 poem of two or more stanzas, what the later stanza adds to the earlier. The reader's own view told apart from the narrator's and characters' (RL.3.6) and from an author's in a short article (RI.3.6); the two books compared on theme, setting and plot (RL.3.9). Writing: a book review recommending the series or not, with reasons in order, linking words and a conclusion (W.3.1a to d), taken through the process, with titles capitalized (L.3.2a); a scene from book 1 rewritten (review of e3-narrative). Speaking: the revised scene presented to the class as a report on the book with facts and descriptive details, at a pace listeners can follow (SL.3.4), in complete sentences where the task asks for detail (SL.3.6), read fluently (review of e3-fluency); the scene's spoken delivery set beside its written script, the conventions each uses named (sentences a listener can follow, a speaker's pauses and stress, against the script's punctuation, paragraphs and stage directions), then both rehearsed and edited to fit their own form, on a short paired model and a check where each student names and applies one difference (L.3.3b). Grammar continued from unit 2 on the books' sentences: what each part of speech does in a sentence (L.3.1a) and subject-verb and pronoun agreement (L.3.1f). A text-based discussion of the class's opinions. Review: e3-character-actions and e3-literal-nonliteral on the book. The unit's word list, studied and spelled. | The base text's module 3, with its classic replaced by an original two-book series (rule 29; the progression stitch, item 6). It needs unit 1's character work and unit 2's narrative arc and figurative language; the book review is the year's first opinion piece, built on unit 2's reasons-and-links work. The base text's performance task here is the presented scene (SL.3.4, SL.3.6) and its book-review revision assesses L.3.3b, so e3-speaking-reports sits here. One long text read whole | Candidate: rubric 2a (one long text read whole, with a reason for its place), 2j (a speaking task with look-fors: the presented scene) | The series is written to the grade 3 text spec; a family or school problem in it is handled gently. The optional classic for independent reading is The Wonderful Wizard of Oz (1900), not Peter Pan, whose "tribe" chapters stereotype Native people. Flagged for the sensitivity read |
| 4 | Water around the world. "Why does clean freshwater matter, and how can people protect it?" | 37 | e3-compare-two-texts | RI.3.9 | Not used (ELA). Leans on science grade 2 (where water is found) | Anchors: an original Curio Class informational book on water on Earth (measure target about 700 to 820); two paired texts on the same topic, the anchor on how far people in one named region walk for water today and a paired text on how a US town cleans its water (federal water-agency pages, public domain); the base text's federal survey excerpt on population growth and water; an original story of a girl whose village gets a new well, read aloud. The most important points and key details of two texts compared in a table of points both make and points only one makes (RI.3.9). Three water issues (access, demand, pollution) researched from given sources (review of e3-short-research and e3-text-features); an opinion essay on why saving water matters, reasons from the texts (review of e3-opinion); a letter to a local water office, with commas in the address (L.3.2b; review of e3-informative). Grammar continued from unit 2 on the water texts: regular and irregular plurals (L.3.1b) and abstract nouns such as pollution and conservation (1c), with tenses (1e, the announcement's "we will") and comparatives (1g, "cleaner", "the most water") used again; parts of speech (1a) checked again. The year's spoken product: a water announcement (a public service message) on one issue, written, rehearsed and recorded as audio on a classroom device, then presented live with a reflection (review of e3-speaking-reports: SL.3.4, SL.3.6, and the script set beside its delivery, L.3.3b). Review: e3-text-connections (what happens when a well is dug), e3-point-of-view (what the author wants readers to believe about water use). The unit's word list, studied and spelled. | The base text's module 4, last because it draws on every routine of the year: research (unit 2), opinion and the spoken report (unit 3), two texts compared, and the year's spoken product. It owns few new rows: its 37 days are the base module's practice and the year-end product, lesson for lesson. Two base lessons fewer: the announcement is recorded as audio on a teacher device in one day, not filmed and edited as video | Candidate: rubric 2v (an end task joining reading, writing and speaking on the topic, with scoring guidance), 2o (writing in one sitting and through the process both in the unit) | Water scarcity and disease named plainly; no student is asked about the family's water or bills, and a class that has lived through a water crisis gets a teacher note before the unit. Places named and shown with their own people's solutions, not as helpless. The action stays an announcement; the class chooses its issue. Flagged for the sensitivity read (the study named in the brief) |

