Session 1 (device): a worked block program for a game that combines an event (when start is clicked), a sequence, a repeat and a choice (if touching the cone, turn around; else move forward), shown in the block-and-text-view (blocks beside their text, read, not typed), traced and predicted before it runs, then the same program with its blocks shuffled to put back in order. Retrieval opening: what does this event-and-repeat program do (grade 2). Session 2 (modify, device): change the question in the choice, the repeat count or one path, predict, run and compare. Session 3 (make, device): pairs build their own unit 2 algorithm (or a classmate's, with credit) as a block program that uses the structures it planned; the program's first comment line names who wrote the algorithm and who built it. Session 4 (device): pairs trade programs and give feedback with a look-for form (does it do what the plan says, is it clear, one suggestion), take at least one suggestion, change the program, test again and write "what I tested and what I changed"; a pair need not take every suggestion. Session 5 (device or printout): given a short program of ten lines at most in the block-and-text-view, say what one marked part is for in the whole program and what would stop working if it were taken out, tracing by pointing at each step; a worked example is read aloud first, then a partner pair reads a second. Retrieval opening: "First I ___, then I ___" for the session 3 program (grade 2). Session 6 (device or printout): a given game program with a score and a counter; children name each variable, say what is stored in it and what changes it, notice that a variable keeps one name spelled the same every time (a misspelled name makes a second box), and fill a trace-table by hand, one column per variable and one row per step; "score gets score plus 1" is read as take what is in score and put one more into it. Retrieval opening: the named box (grade 2). Session 7 (device): given programs, each with one bug, that together cover all four structures (two blocks in the wrong order, a character that does not move when clicked, a repeat that runs too many times, an if that triggers the wrong action or both paths running); trace with a trace-table where a variable is involved, find the first place it goes wrong, change one thing, test again. Out: no typed text code, no data types, no writing variable code (children identify variables in completed programs), no if inside an if or repeat inside a repeat, no functions, no multilevel project, no formal code review, no reading of error reports, no fixing more than one bug at a time; no single program is required to hold every structure, but the unit's programs hold all four. End-of-unit (day 20): a written sitting with a new short program in the block-and-text-view: say what a marked part is for and what would stop working without it (E3-PRO-RD-07); name its variables, what each stores and what changes it, and fill a four-row trace-table (E3-PRO-VD-06); find and fix the bug in a new program whose if triggers the wrong action and in a second whose repeat runs too many times, on paper (E3-PRO-TR-08); scored beside it, each pair's saved program from sessions 3 and 4 against its look-fors (built from a child-created algorithm, uses its planned structures, credit line present; E3-PRO-PD-04) and each child's own line naming the suggestion taken and the change made (E3-PRO-PD-05); respond day 21. Tools: a block editor with the block-and-text-view (blocks with icons and short words, event, repeat, if and if-else blocks, a step-through run that highlights each block and shows each variable's value, job: build, trace and debug the program the step-cards planned; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md; our own editor, STANDARD.md "Our own editor", grades 3 to 5 first). Curio Class writes: the worked, shuffled, modify, read, variable and buggy programs with their keys and the exact message the tool shows for each bug, the paper block cards with if and else cards, the feedback look-for form, the trace-table sheet
