# Course frame: Computer Science, Grade 2

Course `computer-science-grade-2` (catalogue id `cs-k12`), grade 2, one year. Draft frame, 2026-11-25, written on the shape of the kindergarten pilot (docs/courses/computer-science-kindergarten/frame.md and its fix report); the scope and sequence sets the days later.

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 5, one for each concept area at its grade 2 depth, in the order the ideas build: inputs, outputs and
  our choices online (systems and security, so the input-to-output picture and the device rules sit before the first
  program), steps that repeat (algorithms, the first event and repeat on step-cards, and whether a rule is fair),
  programs that repeat (programming, turning unit 2's step-cards into block programs, then debugging and explaining
  them), asking fair questions and training a sorter (data, with machine learning on the class's own data cards),
  and people who made computing tools (computing and society, the year's closer).
- **Days.** 32 course days: 28 in units, 2 for course-level checks (1 after unit 2, 1 after unit 4) and 2 respond
  days (one after each check). One lesson a week of about 30 minutes, 32 weeks: the computer science default for
  kindergarten to grade 2 (docs/courses/computer-science/STANDARD.md section 2, Cory, Desk q-fix-cs-1), a specials
  rotation, so course day n falls in week n. The 30 minutes hold set-up, clean-up and collecting work: a session opens
  with a three-minute retrieval task from the second session on (APPROACH.md, Pacing), and floor-grid and device
  sessions keep four minutes at the end to put cards, tape and devices away, so a session teaches for about 23
  minutes. Each unit's days include its end-of-unit assessment and end with a respond day (APPROACH.md, Pacing), with
  the pilot's one shape for a unit that a course-level check follows directly: in units 2 and 4 the check stands for
  the unit's own end-of-unit assessment and the check's respond day is the unit's respond day (pilot For the lead 2).
  So units 1 to 5 teach on 4, 4, 6, 5 and 3 days, 22 of the 32. Days follow the load of the rows: unit 3 owns four
  programming rows, three of them with device versions and a worked, blanks, modify and make order, and gets 8 days;
  unit 4 owns three rows and four ids, with a new model (the training set) taught over two sessions, and gets 5;
  unit 5 owns three ids whose boundaries ask for different cases (familiar machines today, tools from other places and
  times, the children's own interests), one session each, and gets 5; unit 2's two rows get 4 teaching sessions, three
  of them for the repeat (trace, modify, make). 32 sessions for 14 rows is the progression read's "about two sessions
  a row", counted with checks (docs/reviews/progression-computer-science-2026-11-19.md). Each course-level check takes
  one check session and one respond session. No unit runs over 25 days, so there are no mid-unit assessments.
- **Check weeks.** The grade 2 core checks take weeks 14 to 16, 18 to 20, 28, 29, 31 and 32 (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md,
  target weeks: ELA 15 to 16 and 32, math 18 to 19 and 29, science 20 and 28, social studies 14 and 31). This course's
  checks sit in week 11 (mid-year, after unit 2) and week 26 (year-end, after unit 4), with their respond days in weeks
  12 and 27 (respond days are not check days). Week 26, not 27, holds the year-end check so that unit 5 has the five
  weeks 28 to 32 its three society ids need, as kindergarten's unit 5 keeps weeks after its year-end check. Unit 3 runs
  across weeks 13 to 20, which include the core check weeks 14 to 16 and 18 to 20, because it holds no course-level
  check; unit 5's own end-of-unit assessment falls in week 31, a unit assessment, not a course-level check. Overlaps
  with other after-core subjects' check weeks are the lead's calendar pass (For the lead 1).
- **Standard.** The 16 E2 ids of the 2026 CSTA PK-12 Computer Science Standards, all taught here (STANDARD.md
  section 2): E2-ALG-PS-01, E2-ALG-ML-02, E2-ALG-IM-03, E2-PRO-PD-04, E2-PRO-VD-05, E2-PRO-RD-06, E2-PRO-TR-07,
  E2-DAT-DC-08, E2-DAT-DI-09, E2-DAT-IM-10, E2-SYS-HW-11, E2-SYS-SE-12, E2-SYS-IM-13, E2-SOC-HI-14, E2-SOC-HU-15,
  E2-SOC-CE-16. Each owner row's boundary was read in the source store's standards PDF for this frame (2026-11-25,
  S:\CurioClass-sources\computer-science\csta-2026-11-21, not copied or quoted), and each is taught at the depth its
  owner row's notes set, in our own words (STANDARD.md "Depth"); "What students learn" says what is out. Plans cite ids
  and state each action in a short phrase of our own (STANDARD.md "Quoting"); the credit line "Standards: 2026 CSTA
  PK-12 Computer Science Standards (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)" goes on any day that leans on a statement. Check set: each unit
  plan checks itself against the computer science bar (docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md part 5, items 1 to 9) and the
  lens (docs/agents/lenses/computer-science.md), and reads the partial base unit it adapts for order; the "Check set
  adds" column names what the writer expects that to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds. No Specialty
  id, and no X + CS id, is claimed.
- **Practices and concept areas.** The Units table's fifth column (the C3 column of the core frames) names, for
  computer science, the concept area each unit teaches and the CSTA practices it teaches inside the content, by code
  and our label (STANDARD.md section 3), as bar items 1 and 2 ask. Each unit plan names the practice's day and task.
  Across the year: CT6 define the problem (units 2, 3, 4), CT7 find patterns and make reusable parts (units 3, 4),
  CT8 build and plan (units 2, 3), CT9 test, debug and refine (unit 3), IC3 explain and give feedback (units 1 to 5),
  IC5 be a reliable teammate (units 1, 3, driver and navigator), HCD10 learn from and include different users (units
  4, 5), ESR1 weigh who gains and who is harmed (units 1, 2, 4, 5), ESR2 respect people, credit others' work and
  protect information (units 1, 3). Dispositions are named in the unit plans, not here.
- **Base text.** Partial: England's open computing curriculum, the Teach Computing Curriculum, archived edition
  (raspberrypi.org/curriculum/archived, key stage 2, resources dated 2023), the same fixed edition the kindergarten
  frame maps (its For the lead 4). Year 3 is the grade 2 age (Year 1 is kindergarten in the pilot). License as the
  pilot states: the Open Government Licence v3.0 "unless expressly stated otherwise" on the publisher's terms page; the
  archived unit pages carry no license line of their own, so each file's license line is read when the store folder is
  made. Adapted unit by unit and rephrased for an American audience (STANDARD.md section 2, Cory 2026-11-16): US
  spelling, school years named as US grades, no money in pounds, no product or company name, our own browser tool or
  paper in place of the named block language. Four of its six Year 3 units feed units 1, 3 and 4 (see Source map); its
  two creating-media units are left out, and the network half of "Connecting computers" and the branching-database
  half of "Branching databases" are offered to grade 3. Units 2 and 5 are our own. The source's name appears only in a
  day's credit line, once a day. Pending(docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md#teach-computing-curriculum): the source store holds
  no copy of this base, so the Source map below rests on the public unit pages (lesson titles, opened 2026-11-25; the
  lessons' content is read from their titles and unit overviews), and each adapted lesson's check for third-party
  images, text and tool screenshots waits on the store folder (For the lead 2).
- **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md (row "teaching day") defines them, in computer science's words (APPROACH.md):
  Understanding is an unplugged or trace session, the first teaching of a construct or idea (a step-card dance with a
  start card and a repeat card, a part-card sort, a given block program traced before it runs). Fluency is a modify
  session: change one card or one block of a working set or program and check it; grade 2 has a little of it (unit 2
  session 2, unit 3 session 3, and the retrieval openings). Application is a make or design session: write a
  step-card algorithm for a dance or drawing and have a partner follow it (unit 2 session 3), or turn an algorithm into
  a working block program, test it and fix it (unit 3 session 5). Check is the end-of-unit assessment or a course-level
  check; Respond acts on it by error letter. Inside a unit: understanding before modify before make, and no make day
  before a trace or modify day on the same construct.
- **Evidence.** Grade 2 answers by pointing, sorting, ordering, drawing or saying aloud to a partner; the teacher notes
  by exception on a class checklist (APPROACH.md, "The form of daily evidence"); a program is checked on the screen
  or acted out on the paper path during the session. Every end-of-unit assessment is a sitting with unseen items,
  never the unit's own anchor: a whole-class paper task of about 15 minutes (sort new part cards, lay block cards for
  a new algorithm, circle the first wrong block, sort new data cards) collected from every child, plus one spoken
  answer per child heard in five groups of six while the others finish the paper task. Each owned id has its own
  look-for: a "create" id (E2-ALG-PS-01, E2-PRO-PD-04) is met by a child arranging an algorithm or a program, an
  "explain" or "describe" id (E2-SYS-HW-11, E2-ALG-IM-03, E2-PRO-RD-06, E2-SYS-SE-12) by a spoken answer, a "develop
  a question" id (E2-DAT-DI-09) by the child's own refined question, never by recognition of a supplied one, and a
  "debug" id (E2-PRO-TR-07) by a child finding and fixing one bug. Units 2 and 4 are assessed in the course-level
  check that follows them (Days). No score comes from a session that first teaches a row. The respond day reteaches by
  error letter and repeats the missed item in a new form.
- **Devices.** One device per pair is the default on a device day, never needed per child. Device days: unit 1 session
  2 (one press or one spoken word traced from input to output), unit 3 sessions 2, 3 and 5 (the block program with an
  event and a repeat: traced, modified, then made), with unit 3 session 4 (debugging) on a device or on the floor grid,
  and one optional one-screen sorting tool shown in unit 4 session 5. That leaves about seven eighths of the year's
  time away from a device, more than APPROACH.md's "about two thirds" (ours, untested): the E2 rows are written
  unplugged first, and the device days carry the parts that gain from a screen. Every device day is written four ways
  (STANDARD.md "Devices and rooms"): one device per pair, a few devices at stations with the other pairs on the paper
  version of the same idea, one screen with a child at the device for each step, or no device. No E2 id needs a
  device to be met in full: the boundary of E2-PRO-PD-04 counts coding a robot to dance from an algorithm of arrows,
  so picture-block cards with an event card and a repeat card, run by a partner acting as the robot on the floor grid,
  meet E2-PRO-PD-04 and E2-PRO-TR-07 in full on the paper path; picture cards of parts and a pictured press-to-screen
  chain meet E2-SYS-HW-11; no E2 boundary asks for a computational tool. So the course record never marks a grade 2
  id part met for lack of a device.
- **Paper path.** Every session runs on paper, cards, tape and objects; the block programs of unit 3 have the
  picture-block cards beside them, laid and acted out on the same floor grid, and unit 4's sorter is the class itself
  acting as the machine. No tool asks for a student account, stores a child's name or sends anything off the device
  (STANDARD.md "Privacy and safety").
- **Reading level and vocabulary.** Grade 2 children read simple words; no code is read alone (APPROACH.md, Reading
  load): blocks and cards carry an icon and a short word, every card is read aloud, and the teacher writes longer
  text. Each unit names five to eight words taught in depth, with the everyday words that have a computing meaning
  taught as two meanings (loop, bug, run, label, train). The words input and output are taught with the part cards in
  unit 1 (E2-SYS-HW-11 asks for the right names); repeat and loop in unit 2; the word variable is not used (the E2-PRO-VD-05
  boundary keeps programming words out): a named box is "a box with a label". Sentence frames: "First I ___, then I
  ___" and "I predict it will ___ because ___". A child may answer with arrows, pointing, drawing or the home language.
- **Swap or leave out.** STANDARD.md section 3 lets a school swap or leave out a named unit for its community's
  reasons. In this course every unit carries the only ids of its concept area, so units are swapped, not left out:
  unit 5's cases may be swapped for tools the school's own community made or uses (the same three ids, one case a
  session), and unit 4's question prompts for other neutral class prompts, so the course always claims the data ids
  and the society ids (the bar part 5 fails a course with nothing on data or society). Unit 1's pretend page may be
  swapped for another pretend page on the same two consequences. Units 2 and 3 carry the step-card and block-program
  models the rest of the year and grade 3 use and are not swapped. A school that leaves out a unit anyway names in the
  course page and the unit's family note the ids not claimed that year.
- **Safety.** No lab or field work, so no laboratory or field safety guide applies. Floor-grid sessions run in a
  cleared space with tape that lies flat, children walk the grid (no running, no blindfolds), and device cords stay
  behind the table; devices are carried with two hands. Unit 2's dance algorithms use moves done in place (clap, stomp,
  turn, wave), never a spin with eyes closed. The scope and sequence copies these lines into each floor-grid, dance and
  device day.
- **Costs.** Provisional, for a class of 30, beyond the school's own devices: unit 1 $4 (printing part cards,
  system-diagram mats, benefit-risk charts and the paper pretend page), unit 2 $4 (step-card sets with start and repeat
  cards), unit 3 $12 (two rolls of masking tape for a floor grid, paper cups and labels for the named boxes, printing
  the picture-block cards with event and repeat cards), unit 4 $5 (data cards, data-table sheets, the sorter's picture
  card sets), unit 5 $3 (printing case cards, the timeline strip and interest cards), about $28 in all.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md);
  no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Unit 1 has online-safety content and screen-time harms: the agent
  checks the online-consequences section carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word
  (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md; APPROACH.md, Sensitive ground) and that no card asks what devices or apps a child
  has at home. Unit 2's fairness session touches disability (a rule that needs a fast runner): before it reads, the
  agent studies the National Center on Disability and Journalism's Disability Language Style Guide (ncdj.org/style-guide/)
| 1 | Inputs, outputs and our choices online. *How does a device know what we did, and what happens after we use one?* Anchor: the teacher presses one key and a letter appears on the class screen, then asks what happened in between | 6 | g2-io-parts; g2-benefits-harms; g2-online-consequences | E2-SYS-HW-11; E2-SYS-IM-13; E2-SYS-SE-12 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: IC3 (explain how the parts work together, say what happens next); ESR1 (weigh how a tool helps and how it can hurt); ESR2 (respect people online, credit others' work); IC5 (pair roles at the device) | Session 1: the parts of a device named with their right words (keyboard, mouse, touch screen, microphone, camera, light sensor; screen, speaker, headphones, printer) and sorted as input (takes in from the world) or output (shows or sounds to people), opening with the retrieval of grade 1's part names; the system-diagram introduced: an input part, the computer box, an output part. Session 2 (device): pairs trace one action from input to output on a device (a key press to a letter on the screen, a spoken word to a sound bar moving) and lay it on the system-diagram, saying how the parts work together. Session 3: the benefit-risk-chart for one pictured tool (a video call with a grandparent, a drawing tool, a game): how it helps (learning, fun, being with family) and how it can hurt (tired eyes and a grumpy mood after too long, feeling sad or left out, seeing something scary), and what a person could do about the harm. Session 4: on a paper pretend page, what someone posts or sends is seen by real people and can be kept and passed on; children sort kind and unkind messages and a picture shared without asking, say what happens next to the sender and the reader, and place it on the chart; copying a classmate's picture and posting it as your own is one card, so crediting others' work is named. Out: no ports, no how the computer box processes or stores (the box stays a box), no internet or networks (grade 3), no large-scale harms (the digital divide, false news, data privacy), no laws, no real account or message. End-of-unit (day 5): sort new part cards and place a new action on the system-diagram (spoken: how the parts work together, E2-SYS-HW-11); one benefit and one harm for a new pictured tool and what to do about the harm (E2-SYS-IM-13); a new pretend-page post, said aloud: what happens next and to whom (E2-SYS-SE-12); respond day 6. Tools: device stations (any school device, a text box and a sound-level view on its own screen, job: show one input becoming one output). Curio Class writes: the part cards, the system-diagram mat, the benefit-risk chart, the paper pretend page and its message cards | First because the input-to-output picture and the shared-device rules sit under every device day of unit 3, and the benefit-risk-chart is the model its own session 4 and grade 3 use. Online consequences follow the chart (its model_in) in the same unit. Base: archived Year 3 "Connecting computers" lessons 1 to 3 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "how do digital devices help us" lesson as the helps column of session 3, zero added days | Some homes have no device and some have many: no child is asked what is at home or how long they use screens; the tools are pictured, a child's own example is welcome, never asked for. Screen-time harms are told as feelings anyone can have, not as a family's failing. Online safety: no real name, photo or message is said or written; the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Devices carried with two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 2 | Steps that repeat. *How can a few cards tell a whole dance, and is our rule fair to everyone?* Anchor: the teacher's step-card dance for "when the bell rings" runs long, eight cards of the same clap and stomp, and the class looks for a shorter way | 4 | g2-algorithm-loops; g2-algorithm-peers | E2-ALG-PS-01; E2-ALG-IM-03 | Concept area: algorithms and design. Practices: CT6 (say what the algorithm must do, find the part that repeats); CT8 (plan the cards before a partner follows them); ESR1 (is the rule fair for everyone); IC3 (say why, with a reason) | Session 1: a worked step-card algorithm with a start card (when the bell rings), a sequence and a repeat card that holds the steps it repeats (repeat 3: clap, stomp), traced by the class and then danced; the repeat makes the cards shorter. Retrieval opening: split a pictured task into parts (grade 1). Session 2 (modify): the class changes the repeat count, the start card or one step of the working dance, predicts what will be different and checks by dancing it. Session 3 (make): each pair writes its own step-card algorithm with one start card, a sequence and one repeat, for a dance or a drawing (draw a square: forward, turn, repeat 4), and another pair follows it and says whether it worked; this algorithm is unit 3's program. Session 4: a classroom rule is an algorithm (who is line leader: the first one to the door; a tag-free game where only the fastest runner can win); children play or act it out as written, then judge with three simple tests whether it is fair: does everyone have an equal chance, is anyone left out, could one child win every time while another never gets a turn; the same rule can work well for one child and badly for another. Out: no nested repeat and no repeat inside a repeat, no if or choice (grade 3), no named box in an algorithm, no designing a fairer rule (not asked in grade 2), no talk of bias or how a computer runs a rule. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (day 11) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the dance and drawing step-card sets with start and repeat cards, the line-leader and game rule cards, a short read-aloud about a class that changes how it picks a helper | Second because unit 3's programs are built from these algorithms (g2-code-loops' model_in is the step-card), so the repeat is understood on cards before it is a block. The fairness session sits with the algorithm it judges, and gives unit 4's "who is left out" its first words. Our own (the partial base's Year 3 has no unplugged algorithm unit with repeat) | Candidate: kept, a drawing algorithm (a square) beside the dance for children who cannot do a move, zero added days | Dance moves are done in place, and a child may do any move seated or with hands only; no move is required of a child's body. The fast-runner rule: talk stays on the rule, never on a classmate who is slower or who uses a wheelchair; the teacher picks the game so no child is singled out, and uses the wording the sensitivity read settles from the NCDJ guide. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 3 | Programs that repeat. *How do we turn our dance cards into a program, and what do we do when it repeats too much?* Anchor: the class's step-card dance from unit 2, run on the class screen as blocks, where the character claps one time too many | 8 | g2-name-and-label; g2-code-loops; g2-debug-loops; g2-explain-build | E2-PRO-VD-05; E2-PRO-PD-04; E2-PRO-TR-07; E2-PRO-RD-06 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT7 (the repeat block as a reusable part, the label as a name for a value); CT8 (build from the plan); CT6 and CT9 (find the first wrong block, change one thing, test again); IC3 (explain the steps taken); ESR2 (say which part was your own idea and which a classmate or the teacher helped with); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch at the halfway signal) | Session 1: things that hold information get a name and a value that stays the same or changes (the score in a game, the day of the week, the temperature outside, a pet's name), modeled as named boxes: a paper cup with a label holding one value card; children label pictured pieces of information and say stays or changes. Retrieval opening: changes or stays (grade 1). Session 2 (device): a worked block program with an event block, a sequence and a repeat block, built from a unit 2 step-card dance, traced and predicted before it runs, then the same program with blanks to fill; the score on its screen is labeled as a named box. Session 3 (modify, device): change the repeat count or the event, predict, run and compare. Session 4 (device or floor grid): given programs with one bug each (a repeat that runs too many or too few times, so a square has three sides; an event that never starts the program; two blocks in the wrong order); predict, run, find the first place it goes wrong, change one block, test again; tracing by pointing and counting passes on fingers. Session 5 (make, device): pairs turn their own unit 2 algorithm (or a given one) into a working block program with an event, a sequence and a repeat, test it and fix it. Session 6: each child tells the steps taken to make the session 5 program (the plan, what was built first, what was tested, what changed, what each block does) with "First I ___, then I ___", and says which part was their own idea and which a classmate or the teacher helped with. Out: no if or choice, no repeat inside a repeat, no variable in code and no programming word for the named box (the boxes label things in life and on the screen), no reading of code words alone, no fixing more than one bug at once, no written documentation. End-of-unit (day 19): label three new pieces of information with a name and stays or changes (E2-PRO-VD-05); lay block cards for a new given algorithm with an event and a repeat, acted out by the teacher (E2-PRO-PD-04); find and fix the one bug in a new program (E2-PRO-TR-07); spoken, the steps taken to build the program just laid and whose idea each part was (E2-PRO-RD-06); respond day 20. Tools: a picture-block editor (blocks with icons and short words, an event block and a repeat block, a step-through run that counts each pass of the repeat, job: build, trace and debug the program the step-cards planned; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the named-box cups' label and value cards, the picture-block cards for the paper path, the worked, blanks, modify and buggy programs with their keys, the explain-the-steps frame card | After unit 2 because g2-code-loops' model_in is the step-card algorithm made there, and the repeat is understood on cards first. The named box opens the unit so the score on the program's screen can be labeled. Trace and predict, then blanks, then modify, then debug, then make, then explain the make (APPROACH.md order; the explain row needs a program the child built). Placed across weeks 13 to 20, which include the core check weeks 14 to 16 and 18 to 20, because it holds no course-level check. Base: archived Year 3 "Sequencing sounds" lessons 1 to 4 and "Events and actions in programs" lessons 3, 5 and 6 (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "debugging movement" lesson order (test, spot, fix) inside session 4, zero added days | Floor grid in a cleared space, tape flat, children walk (no running, no blindfolds); a child who cannot walk the grid moves a toy or directs a partner, never sits out. The repeat count stays at 2 to 4, counted pass by pass, so no child needs multiplication (see Cross-subject). The "whose idea" line is credit, not blame: helped parts are praised. A pet's name is one card among many; no child is asked about their own pets. Devices: two hands, cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 4 | Fair questions and a sorting machine. *Which question should we ask our class, and how could a sorting machine get it wrong?* Anchor: a sorter that learned only from pictures of birds with wings spread calls a sitting bird "not a bird" | 5 | g2-data-types; g2-data-questions; g2-ai-training-data | E2-DAT-DC-08; E2-DAT-DI-09; E2-DAT-IM-10; E2-ALG-ML-02 | Concept area: data and analysis, with algorithms (machine learning). Practices: CT6 (refine a question data can answer); CT7 (patterns in the table and in the examples); IC3 (say what the data tells); ESR1 (an unfair way of asking, an uneven sorter); HCD10 (who is left out by a way of asking) | Session 1: number data (how tall the bean plant is, how many crayons in a box) and word data (the color of a leaf, the shape of a block): how each is collected (measure, count, ask, look) and what each tells (number data compares and adds, word data sorts and describes); both go into a two-column paper data-table, rows and columns, beside a picture-graph of the same data, with simple comparisons (more leaves are yellow than red). Retrieval opening: count or description (grade 1). Session 2: children turn a vague question into one data can answer (which games do kids like becomes which game does our class pick most for indoor recess) and set aside a question data cannot answer well (which game is the best one); each pair refines its own question from a picture prompt. Session 3: ways of collecting are compared (ask only the front row, ask everyone, hands up or a secret vote): which could give a wrong or unfair answer and who it leaves out, in the class, the school or the neighborhood, and how a one-sided way of asking leads to an unfair choice (a recess game picked by the front row only); no new data is collected. Session 4: a class sorter learns from labeled example cards: the training set; the children act as the machine, study the labeled examples and then guess new cards. Retrieval opening: guess a new card's group from three examples (grade 1). Session 5: the same sorter trained on a set that was mostly one kind (every bird with wings spread) makes more mistakes on the kind it saw least; children say why and that a sorter with uneven examples can give uneven or unfair results; a device sorting tool may be shown on one screen. Out: no mean, median or scaled graph, no large data set, no device for collecting, no child trains a machine model alone, no claim that a sorter thinks, no data about named children. End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 26) stands for it. Tools: optional one-screen picture sorter (trained by the teacher in the room on picture cards, nothing stored or sent, job: show a sorter's guesses on new pictures). Curio Class writes: the data cards, the data-table sheet, the question picture prompts, the bird and leaf training sets (one balanced, one uneven) and their new-card sets, all drawn by Curio Class | After unit 3 and before the year's check, and after math grade 2's picture and bar graphs (see Cross-subject). The data-table comes first, then questions of it, then how asking can leave people out, so the training set arrives as data that can also leave a kind out. Machine learning sits in the data unit because its grade 2 boundary is about the data a model is trained on. Base: archived Year 3 "Branching databases" lessons 1 and 2 (yes or no questions about attributes, making groups) for session 4's sorting (Source map) | Candidate: kept, the partial base's "yes or no questions" as the sorter's look-for in session 4, zero added days | Class data is about things, never people: no food, pet, home or body questions; the teacher picks prompts from neutral lists, and the bean plant and crayons are class items. No vote or answer is linked to a named child. The sorter's mistakes are about pictures of birds and leaves, never people or faces. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
| 5 | People who made computing tools. *Why did people in different places and times make tools for counting, writing and moving safely, and which tools are linked to what we like?* Anchor: a traffic light at the school crossing, and the question of what went wrong before there was one | 5 | g2-people-solve; g2-interests-careers | E2-SOC-HU-15; E2-SOC-HI-14; E2-SOC-CE-16 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: ESR1 (who each tool helped); HCD10 (tools made for people with different needs and abilities); IC3 (say what problem each tool solved) | Session 1: familiar machines people made to solve everyday problems (a traffic light, an automatic door, a library's book scanner, a crossing signal that sounds for people who cannot see it, the class timer): for each, children match the problem it was made for (cars and walkers at a crossing, carrying things through a door, keeping track of library books) to the tool, and say who it helps. Retrieval opening: people, computer or both (grade 1). Session 2: case cards of people in different places and times who made tools to count, record, read and share ideas: the counting frame (abacus) used in China and Japan, the knotted-cord records (khipu) of the Andes, and the reading code of raised dots made by Louis Braille, a blind teenager in France; children place them on a timeline strip and say what problem each solved and for whom. Retrieval opening: before and after on the timeline (grade 1). The teacher's facts come from the case sources (Sensitivity reads; the Computer History Museum timeline, computerhistory.org/timeline/, for the abacus; the National Federation of the Blind's braille pages, nfb.org, for the reading code), checked when the cards are written. Session 3: each child picks an interest (animals, art, sports, music, building) and finds, on picture cards, a job linked to it where people use computing (a clinic's animal records, drawing pictures on a tablet, tracking a game's scores, recording a song), then draws and tells it. Out: no how the tools work inside, no dates beyond the order on the strip, no history of computing as a whole, no comparison of cultures' impacts, no job qualifications or named tools, no named products or companies. End-of-unit (day 31): match two new familiar machines to the problem each solves (E2-SOC-HU-15); place a new case card on the timeline and say what problem the tool solved for its people (E2-SOC-HI-14); spoken, one interest and one way computing is used in a job linked to it (E2-SOC-CE-16); respond day 32 | Last because it gathers the year: the input and output of unit 1 seen in machines that help people, and the algorithms and programs of units 2 and 3 seen as things people made. Its rows need nothing taught after them and hand on only to grade 3, so it can follow the year-end check in week 26, and its five weeks fit before the year's end. Our own (the partial base's Year 3 has no society unit) | Candidate: dropped, a visit by a school worker who uses computing for a job; picture cards carry the row and a visit needs a school's own arrangement, zero days | Khipu is Native Andean knowledge: told as the record-keeping of the Inca and other Andean peoples, not as a lost mystery, and as part of Andean life today where the sources say so; use the wording the sensitivity read settles from its pack. Braille is told as a tool a blind person made for blind readers; no child is asked to act blind or wear a blindfold. No child is asked about a family member's job; a child's own offer is welcome. Pictures show many kinds of work, people and places, rural and town. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first). |

